{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command '\"'$l=Get-Content -LiteralPath \"/home/sharpninja/.claude/plans/create-a-new-plan-giggly-music.md\"; for($i=111;$i -le 220 -and $i -le $l.Count;$i++){ \"{0,3}: {1}\" -f $i,$l[$i-1] }'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"111: \n112: Clearance (operator, 2026-10-09: \"Partial clearance\"), posted as an ANSWER in `MEMORY-PROCESS-007` at 18:08:08Z: QB-AGENT-001 P1 may continue on REPL pinning and exclusive REPL ownership, isolated child credentials, workspace-bound bootstrap, typed lifecycle failures, and the audit-outbox storage internals (`src/runtime/core-config.ts`, `src/discovery/marker-resolver.ts`, `src/transport/repl-bridge.ts`, `src/runtime/core-runtime-error.ts`, the persistence half of `src/cache/audit-outbox.ts`); the turn lifecycle in `host-context.ts`, the session lifecycle in `session.ts` and `session-shim.ts`, and the audit event and receipt shape stay on HOLD until Codex ACKs C1-C5 and C9 or the operator resolves a REQUEST. That ANSWER left slice-turn hostile reviews (its item 5) open; operator D10 settled it, and a NOTICE at 19:51:42Z posted D9, D10, D11, D12 and the proposed C9 code additions. As of 2026-10-09T19:52Z Codex had posted no ACK; P0.7 re-reads the channel and records each contract's disposition.\n113: \n114: Contract-disposition gate: no slice that changes `host-context.ts`, `session.ts`, `session-shim.ts`, the audit-outbox event or receipt shape, or the shared server session-log files is integrated or merged until Codex has posted ACK for C1-C5 and C9 in `MEMORY-PROCESS-007`, or the operator has resolved a REQUEST about each one not ACKed; each of C6-C8 and C10-C12 must be ACKed or resolved the same way before the slice that depends on it starts (C6 and C7: A6 and A8; C8: S10; C10: S9; C11: S4 and S5; C12: S6). Sequencing (order-independent): P0.7 records QB-AGENT-001's state from the channel (merged to `develop` or not, SHA). A slice that touches a shared file starts from current `develop`: if QB-AGENT-001 P1 has merged, the slice builds on it; if not, the slice proceeds and QB-AGENT-001 rebases. In both orders the contracts below hold; where landed QB-AGENT-001 code contradicts one, the slice records a RequirementRefined decision, posts a CONTRACT entry, and implements the contract on top of that code without weakening any FR of this plan. Phase B's S4 and S5 start only after a READINESS entry for QB-AGENT-001 P2 or an operator ruling posted as REQUEST/ANSWER, because both change the session-turn read surfaces.\n115: \n116: Shared contracts (posted as the first CONTRACT entry in P0.7):\n117: - C1 Multiple open turns per host session: a map of open turns by requestId plus a per-host-session write target (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC005, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-003, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-007); no single current-turn slot; \"finish before another starts\" applies per turn, never per session.\n118: - C2 Server-minted turn identity: requestIds come from the mint route (D1); an event recorded before the mint returns carries the turn's `ingressNonce` and the provisional `pending-<nonce>` id, and replay rebinds every such event to the minted requestId atomically and in original order (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC008).\n119: - C3 Outcome mapping into any Node persistence path, including an audit outbox (TR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-008 AC-006): `created` and `merged` -> `persisted`; `skipped_duplicate` -> `unchanged`; `skipped_terminal` -> `rejected` with reason `skipped_terminal`, never retried or quarantined, failsafe record cleared; a server without per-turn outcomes -> `outcomeVerified:false`.\n120: - C4 Every persisted or queued event carries its immutable D2 binding `{ hostSessionKey, keySource, requestId, boundAtUtc }`; replay never re-resolves it.\n121: - C5 State is scoped per host session key (host binding table), not per process; one process may serve several host sessions (cline-v2 conversations); REPL ownership may stay per process.\n122: - C6 Action types come only from `docs/context/action-types.md`; where code in this plan records a tool run it uses dialog items with category `tool_call`/`tool_result`; adding `tool_execution` needs its own requirement, doc change, and server validation first.\n123: - C7 Each decision is written as the workspace triple: a `designDecisions` entry, a dialog item with category `decision`, and a `design_decision` action.\n124: - C8 Completion: after S10 a completion without `requestClass` and terminal reviews is refused with 400 `session_turn_completion_gate`; callers treat it as a final, non-retried failure and the turn stays in progress; hooks never complete turns.\n125: - C9 Typed failures carry the server or plugin detail code (`turn_immutable`, `skipped_terminal`, `host_session_unknown`, `host_session_ambiguous`, `request_ambiguous`, `request_unknown`, `turn_logging_unsupported`, `mint_requires_host_ingress`, `nonce_source_missing`, `session_turn_completion_gate`, `request_class_invalid`, `review_*`, `ingress_nonce_invalid`) so callers can tell retryable from final failures.\n126: - C10 Review scores travel as their original text: Node never converts them to JS numbers (the D12 raw-token parser reads them as exact strings on Node 18 and later), and any event hash is computed over the original text, never over a re-serialized parsed value.\n127: - C11 Paginated turn listing and detail reads return `requestClass`, `agentReview`, and `hostileReview`, sanitized as FR-MCP-SESSIONLOGSAN-001 requires, in a stable order.\n128: - C12 The pinned REPL includes `workflow.sessionlog.mintTurn`, `workflow.sessionlog.recordReview`, and the lossless score normalizer (S6) before any host pins it.\n129: \n130: ## BDPv4 process contract (applies to every slice)\n131: \n132: Sources: `docs/Development-Process-draft-v4.md:76` (tests validated with mocks before real code); `docs/Byrd-Dev-Process-v4-Project-Management-Guide.md` 5.1 status machine, 5.2 handoff contract, 5.3 test gate, line 221 loop \"Red -> mocks-green -> real Green -> refactor -> full unit gate -> Validating\"; `skills/byrd-tdd-process/SKILL.md`.\n133: \n134: Requirements DB is authoritative; `docs/Project/*.md` is a generated projection (regenerated, never hand-edited). This overrides the SKILL step 1 wording (\"append entries to docs/Project\").\n135: \n136: Per slice, in order:\n137: 1. Contract first: interfaces, DTO members, and skeletons returning neutral values (empty/null/false, never throwing) so RED fails on an assertion. Compile-failure RED only for the first stub of a phase. PowerShell stubs return `$null`/`$false`/`@()`.\n138: 2. RED: write the named tests from the ACs; run; record command, `Failed: N, Passed: M, Skipped: 0`, and the first assertion message as a TODO TestDefined checkpoint.\n139: 3. MOCKS-GREEN: make the same tests pass against mocks only, no production logic. Pure seams use the CT pattern: abstract `XxxContractTests` with `_Oracle` (hand-written double), `_Production`, and permanent `XxxMutantDetectionTests` (a deliberately wrong double must fail every assertion). Glue slices substitute the seams. Negative check every slice: a wrong mock value makes the target test fail (recorded). Tooling: NSubstitute (Support.Mcp.Tests, Repl.Core.Tests, Support.Mcp.IntegrationTests); hand-written doubles in Client.Tests (`MockHttpHandler`) and McpAgent.Tests; Pester `Mock` plus seams `MCP_PLUGIN_REPL_LOG`/`MCP_PLUGIN_REPL_RESPONSE` (`plugin-hook.ps1:216-227`) and `MCP_PLUGIN_PERSIST_LOG` (`repl-invoke.ps1:1624-1640`).\n140: 4. Agent self-review of RED and mocks-green (recorded on the slice's session turn as decisions; after S8 also as `agentReview`) before any production change. No hostile review before the slice is all green (D10).\n141: 5. REAL GREEN: production code; state which tests stay contract tests.\n142: 6. REFACTOR: delete `_Oracle` subclasses, keep mutant tests; clean production code.\n143: 7. Gates G1-G6 (zero failures, zero skips):\n144:    - G1 targeted `dotnet test <proj>.csproj -c Debug --filter \"<filters>\"` and/or targeted Pester.\n145:    - G2 `./build.ps1 Test` (it excludes `*IntegrationTests`, `*PluginIntegration*`, `*.Validation`, `*Review.Tests`, and `Build.Tests`, `build/Build.Test.cs:18-24`; slices that change `build/` code also run `dotnet test tests/Build.Tests/Build.Tests.csproj -c Debug`).\n146:    - G3 after G2 green, the integration set the slice names: `tests/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.IntegrationTests`, `tests/QBrainAi.SessionLog.Validation`, `./build.ps1 MigrationIntegrationTests`, `./build.ps1 PluginSessionLogIntegration`, `tests/QBrainAi.Repl.IntegrationTests`, `tests/QBrainAi.Acid.IntegrationTests`.\n147:    - G4 plugin core: `pwsh -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command \"Invoke-Pester -Path 'plugins/core/test-fixtures/pester/PluginPowerShellRuntime.Tests.ps1','plugins/core/test-fixtures/pester/<touched>.Tests.ps1' -CI\"`.\n148:    - G5 `./build.ps1 ValidateConfig` exits 0; `./build.ps1 ValidateTraceability` exits 0 (it fails on any FR missing from the mapping, `build/Build.ValidateTraceability.cs:69-74`, so every catalog FR must be mapped). Phase exits (P0, A9, S11) add `./build.ps1 ValidateTraceability --strict-tr-and-test-coverage` and the scoped use-case gate (operator D3, 2026-10-09: the use-case gate checks only this plan's requirements). Pass rule: (a) the strict run's TR/TEST matrix findings contain no catalog id and no finding absent from the P0 baseline list (P0 records the baseline findings before creating anything; the strict run may exit non-zero only because of baseline findings, and the receipt lists them); (b) `client.UseCases.GetCoverageAsync` lists none of the catalog FRs (the 21 new FRs and the updated FRs in \"Updated existing requirements\") in `functionalRequirementsWithoutRealizesUseCase`. The global `--strict-use-case-fr-coverage` flag is not a gate: it covers every FR in the store, and `ValidateTraceability` reads use-case links only from a SQLite path (`UseCaseFrTraceabilityLoader.ResolveDefaultSqlitePath`), so the store-backed `GetCoverageAsync` check is authoritative.\n149:    - G6 per-plugin gates (matrix above).\n150: 8. Agent self-review of the finished slice (slice turn decisions; after S8 also `agentReview`); the slice TODO moves to `Validating`; then the slice's Codex HV runs (D10) and the TODO moves to Complete only after that HV returns AGREE >= 98/98.\n151: \n152: Hostile validation protocol (operator directives 2026-10-09: first \"at completion of major phases\", then refined by D10 to the end of every slice once its gates are green, with HV-P0, HV-A9, and HV-S11 also covering their whole phase; reviewer is Codex CLI, model `gpt-6-sol`, reasoning effort `xhigh`, replacing the earlier PR-comment protocol):\n153: - Major phases and their HV gates: HV-P0 (requirements, use cases, TODOs, plan doc), HV-A (Phase A A1-A9 incl. all plugin repos), HV-B (Phase B S1-S11 incl. all plugin repos). Every slice ends with its own HV once G1-G6 are green (D10): HV-A1..HV-A9 and HV-S1..HV-S11, where HV-A9 also covers all of Phase A (= HV-A) and HV-S11 all of Phase B (= HV-B). Turns of a Code or Docs slice that complete before its HV lands (operator confirmed this interpretation of D10 on 2026-10-09: \"agree\") record `hostileReview` status `Other` with `otherNotes` \"pending slice HV <gate>\"; when the slice HV lands, its verdict mapped as FR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-005-AC002 states (server-derived Pass or Fail from the scores, or `Other` with `otherNotes` naming the failed claims when a DISAGREE has scores that would derive Pass), with both receipt paths is recorded with `recordReview` onto every turn of that slice (reviews stay writable on completed turns), and the slice HV brief lists those turns.\n154: - At each HV point (P0's end, and each slice's end once G1-G6 are green), push the worktree branch (and the branch of each plugin repo the slice or phase changed). Write the HV brief: the claims (with commands, paths, test counts, SHAs, session turn ids), the untrusted implementer receipt, the acceptance rule, mandatory surfaces A-D, the add-profile first action, the review-only scope, and the receipt paths.\n155: - Launch the reviewer with `codex exec -m gpt-6-sol -c model_reasoning_effort=\"xhigh\" --sandbox workspace-write -c sandbox_workspace_write.network_access=true -C <worktree> --json -`, brief on stdin. The reviewer is review-only (it writes only under `docs/receipts/hv/` and to its own MCP session-log turn) and returns claim-by-claim PASS/FAIL/UNKNOWN, `OverallVerdict`, accuracy, completeness, findings, and a `=== VERDICT JSON ===` block. The implementing agent writes the brief and launches the run; it never edits the reviewer's output or verdict. Verify the live model and effort from the Codex session rollout (`turn_context` with `model=gpt-6-sol`, `effort=xhigh`); a run on any other model or effort is not an HV.\n156: - Receipts: the runner writes `docs/receipts/hv/<utc>-<gate>.request.jsonl` (a meta object with the launch command, model, effort, head SHA, and brief SHA-256, plus the full brief body; built from PowerShell objects with `ConvertTo-Json -Compress`) and `.response.jsonl` (the reviewer's complete `--json` event stream). The reviewer writes `hostile-validator-<gate>-<utc>.md` and `.json`. All are committed on the branch. Store the complete verdict in the MCP session log on the turn that launched the HV (the slice's or P0's coordinating turn) (ordered dialog parts that concatenate byte for byte to the markdown receipt, plus designDecisions, the jsonl paths, and the Codex rollout path); the reviewer also stores it on its own turn. After S8 ships, also record it with `workflow.sessionlog.recordReview kind: hostile` (with `requestJsonlPath`/`responseJsonlPath`).\n157: - Pass only with `OverallVerdict AGREE` and accuracy >= 98 and completeness >= 98. On DISAGREE: display every FAIL/UNKNOWN to the operator, fix on the branch, push, and launch a new Codex round whose brief re-attacks the prior receipt; the slice stays open (and, at HV-P0, HV-A9, or HV-S11, its phase).\n158: - Phase PRs against `develop` (McpServer plus each changed plugin repo, cross-linked) cite the HV receipt paths and verdicts. Merge a phase PR only after HV AGREE and operator approval. The next phase starts only after its predecessor has HV AGREE, operator approval, every phase PR merged (McpServer and each changed plugin repo), and the merged heads verified on their default branches (operator phase rule, `MEMORY-PROCESS-006`); P0 likewise completes before Phase A starts.\n159: \n160: TODO transitions (plugin todo workflow / `client.Todo.*`): Planned -> TestDesign (slice start) -> TestReady (RED recorded, `SetTestPlanAsync unitTestsDefined: true`) -> Implementing (mocks-green and negative check recorded, agent self-review recorded) -> Validating (real green, refactor, G1-G6 green, agent self-review of the slice) -> Complete (only after the slice's Codex hostile validation (D10) returns AGREE >= 98/98, recorded via `RecordValidationResultAsync` with the HV receipt paths and jsonl paths); slice DISAGREE -> that slice TODO to Blocked with the findings, fix, re-review; a phase-wide DISAGREE at HV-A9 or HV-S11 blocks every slice it names.\n161: \n162: Session logging during execution: every operator request is its own turn, targeted by explicit `requestId` (`client.SessionLog.BeginTurnAsync/PatchTurnAsync/CompleteTurnAsync`) until Phase A ships; decisions go to `designDecisions`; every write verified server-side with `client.SessionLog.QueryAsync`.\n163: \n164: ## P0: Requirements, use cases, interfaces, TODOs (no product code)\n165: \n166: Baseline already established (2026-10-09, operator-directed, turn `req-20261009T020009Z-prompt-fd7e`): requirements missing from the store were imported from develop `docs/Project/*.md` by non-destructive canonical upsert (`client.Requirements.IngestAsync`, upsert-only per `RequirementsController.cs:847-859`): +13 FR, +18 TR, +19 TEST, +13 mappings, 0 deletions; store now FR 352, TR 480, TEST 512 with 0 develop-doc ids missing. The hosted GitHub wiki (`3f40792`, 2026-09-23) was not imported: it is staler than the store and wiki mode is authoritative (it would have deleted ACKRECOVERY, RUNAS, SERVICEUPDATE, AGENT-PARITY records). Triage filed for the plugin argv limit and the non-atomic ingest.\n167: \n168: 1. Toolchain gate on the execution host, recorded as a PlanningDecision: `dotnet --list-sdks` must include an SDK accepted by `global.json` (`10.0.201`, `rollForward: latestFeature`, so any 10.0.2xx or later feature band; on 2026-10-09 LAB-OMARCHY had only `10.0.111`, which fails `./build.ps1` with \"A compatible .NET SDK was not found\"), Pester 5 must be importable by `pwsh` (none installed on 2026-10-09), `bats` must be on PATH (missing on 2026-10-09), `node`/`npm` must satisfy each TypeScript repo's `engines` (node v26.8.2, npm 11.19.1 present), and `dotnet ef` must run at the EF Core version pinned in `Directory.Packages.props` (`Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design` 10.0.0); on 2026-10-09 `dotnet ef --version` failed and `.config/dotnet-tools.json` pinned only `docfx`. Missing tools are installed only with operator approval and only through PowerShell: the SDK, on this Linux host, by a PowerShell-only download and extract pinned to 10.0.201: `Invoke-WebRequest` of `https://builds.dotnet.microsoft.com/dotnet/Sdk/10.0.201/dotnet-sdk-10.0.201-linux-x64.tar.gz`, a SHA-512 check against the `hash` that `https://builds.dotnet.microsoft.com/dotnet/release-metadata/10.0/releases.json` lists for that file (stop on mismatch), extraction with `tar -xzf` into `$HOME/.dotnet` invoked from PowerShell, `DOTNET_ROOT` and `PATH` set for the session, then `dotnet --list-sdks` showing 10.0.201 (Microsoft documents `dotnet-install.ps1` for Windows and `dotnet-install.sh` for Linux, so neither script is used here); `dotnet-ef` as a local tool pinned in the repo manifest (`dotnet tool install dotnet-ef --version 10.0.0` from the repo root, committed `.config/dotnet-tools.json`, then `dotnet tool restore`), invoked per provider as `dotnet tool run dotnet-ef migrations add <Name> --project src/QBrainAi.Storage.<Provider>Migrations --startup-project src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp --context McpDbContext` with `MCP_EF_PROVIDER` (`sqlite`, `postgresql`, `sqlserver`) and `MCP_EF_MIGRATIONS_ASSEMBLY=QBrainAi.Storage.<Provider>Migrations` read by `src/QBrainAi.Storage/McpDbContextFactory.cs:26-30` (form documented in `docs/Operations/permanent-local-audit-sqlserver.md:19-27`; `postgresql` is the canonical PostgreSQL name, `src/QBrainAi.Storage/Database/PostgreSqlMcpDatabaseProviderStrategy.cs:15`); `Install-Module Pester -MinimumVersion 5.0 -Scope CurrentUser`; `bats` via the distribution package. No gate may be recorded as passed on a host that fails this check. Run a read-only `workflow.triage.getGroup id: triage-group-804b21086f3019ab` with `workspacePath` set to the worktree (the path the report was filed under) and with the main workspace, and record both results (see \"Risks and incidental bugs\"). Record the approval HEAD (`git rev-parse HEAD`) and confirm `git diff --name-only f56dcf70..HEAD` still touches no `src/`, `tests/`, `build/`, or `plugins/` path (otherwise re-verify every source anchor in this plan before continuing). Save this plan as `docs/plans/PLAN-SESSIONTURN-SESSIONREVIEW-001.md`, including the wire contracts below.\n169: 2. Read-only spike, recorded as a PlanningDecision: confirm from the session transcript and server turns how `<task-notification>` and slash commands arrive at UserPromptSubmit and whether each carries its own `prompt_id`; capture a Claude Code transcript excerpt with `queue-operation`/`enqueue` lines as the A3 arrival-bridge fixture (the bridge design does not depend on whether UserPromptSubmit also fires for queued messages, because the hook claims a bridge mint when it does); and settle the four host binding table checks (Cowork executes plugin hooks; Codex sets `CODEX_THREAD_ID` equal to `session_id` in shell commands; Grok sets `GROK_SESSION_ID` in shell commands; a queued cline-v2 message starts its own run at arrival). Each check selects between the outcomes the table already defines; no new design is made, and any host limitation it confirms is shown to the operator.\n170: 3. Re-verify id families are free (`workflow.requirements.getFr/getTr/getTest` not_found): `FR/TR/TEST-MCP-SESSIONTURN-*`, `-SESSIONREVIEW-*`, `-SESSIONCLASS-*`, and `FR-SUPPORT-016`, `TR-SUPPORT-CORE-016`, `TEST-SUPPORT-023`, each also checked against the createBatch id grammar (SESSIONREVIEW/TURNREVIEW verified 0 on 2026-10-09; the store then held FR 352, TR 480, TEST 512).\n171: 4. Create every NEW requirement in the \"Requirements catalog\" section below exactly as listed (21 FR, 23 TR, 23 TEST; ids, titles, statements, ACs) with `workflow.requirements.createBatch` (kind fr|tr|test, area MCP, except the three SUPPORT records (FR-SUPPORT-016, TR-SUPPORT-CORE-016, TEST-SUPPORT-023), which copy the area of FR/TR/TEST-SUPPORT-010F as read with `getFr`/`getTr`/`getTest` first, priority high, `acceptanceCriteria: [{id, text, isSatisfied: false}]`), then one `workflow.requirements.createMapping {frId, trIds, testIds}` per row of \"Mappings\" and per row of \"Mapping additions for updated FRs\", passing that row's full ids literally (`src/QBrainAi.Repl.Core/RequirementsWorkflow.cs:464-510` validates and fetches each exact id). Apply every UPDATED requirement change listed in \"Updated existing requirements\" with `workflow.requirements.updateFr/updateTr/updateTest` (record the before text in the P0 turn). Every FR, TR, and TEST carries ACs.\n172: 5. Create every use case in \"Use cases (new)\" with `client.UseCases.CreateAsync` (title, briefDescription, createBasicFlow, first frId, linkType Realizes), add the remaining Realizes FRs with `client.UseCases.LinkFrAsync`, fill actors, preconditions, basic and alternate flows with `client.UseCases.UpdateAsync`, set approval `Submitted` (operator approves); generate mermaid diagrams (`client.UseCases.GetDiagramAsync format: mermaid`) into the plan doc. Baseline (2026-10-09T03:12Z, `client.UseCases.GetCoverageAsync`, after the develop-docs import): 0 use cases, 352 FRs, 0 linked, 352 FRs without a Realizes use case; there are no existing use cases to update.\n173: 6. Regenerate docs with `workflow.requirements.generateDocument format: markdown docType: fr|tr|test|matrix` (the workflow accepts only `fr|tr|test|matrix|all`, `src/QBrainAi.Repl.Core/RequirementsWorkflow.cs:606-623,873-879`), plus `client.Requirements.GenerateAsync` with docType `mapping` for `TR-per-FR-Mapping.md` (the typed client and controller accept `mapping`, `RequirementsController.cs:935-947`) (write the returned content byte-for-byte into the worktree `docs/Project/*.md` if the server's configured paths are not this worktree), then `format: wiki docType: all`.\n174: 7. Sync or acquire all nine plugin repos per the matrix (local ones: lineage check, tagged stash, fast-forward to the resolved default branch; missing ones: tool-registry acquisition), re-check the host binding table rows and test commands against each head, and record how the packed Node core is pinned in each TypeScript host (`package.json` dependency or vendored `.tgz`). Record versions and heads as a PlanningDecision. Read the coordination channel `MEMORY-PROCESS-007`, record QB-AGENT-001's state (merged to `develop` or not, SHA, latest READINESS), post the C1-C12 CONTRACT entry (see \"Coordination with QB-AGENT-001\"), and record the current disposition of each contract (ACK, ANSWER, open REQUEST, or none yet) as a PlanningDecision.\n175: 8. Create TODOs with `workflow.todo.create` (section SessionLog, priority high, FR/TR arrays, dependsOn): MCP-SESSIONTURN-001..009 (A1..A9), MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-001..011 (S1..S11). Chain: A1 and A2 roots; A3, A4, A6 depend on A1; A5 on A1, A4; A7 on A3, A4; A8 on A1; A9 on A2..A8; S1 on A9; S2 on S1; S3 on S2; S4 on S3; S5 on S4; S6 on S5; S7 on S2, S5; S8 on S6, S7; S9 on S5, S7; S10 on S6, S7, S8, S9; S11 on S10. Append PlanningDecision checkpoints for every locked decision and rejected alternative.\n176: 9. Gate P0: G5 as defined in the BDPv4 contract, including the phase-exit strict TR/TEST run and the scoped use-case gate (operator D3): `client.UseCases.GetCoverageAsync` lists none of the catalog FRs in `functionalRequirementsWithoutRealizesUseCase` (baseline 2026-10-09T03:12Z: 0 use cases, all 352 FRs unlinked; the pre-existing FRs are out of scope by D3). The baseline TR/TEST and FR findings of `ValidateTraceability` are recorded before step 4 creates anything; `./build.ps1 ValidateConfig`; the baseline-green run of `SessionLogServiceReplaceDeleteTests` (TDD AC allocation) with zero failures and zero skips; HV-P0 by Codex gpt-6-sol xhigh against the pushed P0 branch head (claims: ids in store and docs, ACs testable, UCs linked, interfaces documented, dependsOn valid, defect receipts reproducible). TODOs move Draft -> Planned only after HV-P0 AGREE.\n177: \n178: ## Requirements catalog\n179: \n180: AC ids use `<REQID>-AC###`, the format of the SESSIONLIFE family this plan updates (e.g. `FR-MCP-SESSIONLIFE-001-AC004`). New ACs on existing requirements keep that requirement's existing AC format (`ac-N` for FR-SUPPORT-*, SESSIONLOGSAN, TRIAGESTORE, PLUGININT); requirements that today carry only inline prose ACs get structured `<REQID>-AC###` ACs. In the allocation lists, shorthand `SESSIONTURN-004-001` means `FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-004-AC001`. The bracket after each AC names the slice whose RED test first covers it; \"+\" lists later slices that extend coverage on another surface. Each AC is verified by the TEST listed under its TR.\n181: \n182: ### New functional requirements (21)\n183: \n184: FR-SUPPORT-016 Turn replace and section API (created in P0. The behavior is cited in source and tests under the legacy id `FR-SUPPORT-010G`, which the requirements grammar rejects (`src/QBrainAi.Repl.Core/RequirementsWorkflow.cs:32-34,400-406`: the suffix must be three digits), so P0 creates it as FR-SUPPORT-016 with TR-SUPPORT-CORE-016 and TEST-SUPPORT-023 (all three verified free in the live store), and S4, the first slice that edits these files, renames every legacy citation; the legacy id is cited 36 times in 9 files per `rg -n -F FR-SUPPORT-010G src tests`: `src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/SessionLogService.cs` (5, including `TurnSection`/`ParseSection` at 1287-1317), `ISessionLogService.cs` (6), `src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Controllers/SessionLogController.cs` (7), `src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/McpStdio/FwhMcpTools.SessionLog.cs` (6), `src/QBrainAi.Client/SessionLogClient.cs` (7), `src/QBrainAi.Client/Models/SessionLogModels.cs` (1), `tests/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Tests/Services/SessionLogServiceReplaceDeleteTests.cs` (2), and the integration tests `SessionLogControllerTests.cs` (1) and `SessionLogReplaceDeleteControllerTests.cs` (1); also cited by the retired `CODEX-HANDOFF.md:206`, readable with `git show f56dcf70:CODEX-HANDOFF.md`). Statement: the session-log API SHALL support replacing a turn (PUT turn), replacing or resetting one section of a turn (PUT/DELETE section), and deleting one item of a collection section (DELETE item), for the sections actions, tags, context, dialog, commits, designDecisions, requirementsDiscovered, filesModified, blockers, and (after Phase B) agentReview and hostileReview. Same family and AC style as FR-SUPPORT-010E/010F and FR-SUPPORT-014/015 (`ac-N`).\n185: - `ac-1` Replacing a turn clears omitted scalars and collections and preserves the workspace stamp on children. [existing behavior; verified in P0 by `SessionLogServiceReplaceDeleteTests.ReplaceTurnAsync_ClearsOmittedScalarsAndCollections`, `ReplaceTurnAsync_PreservesWorkspaceStampOnChildren`]\n186: - `ac-2` Replacing a section changes only that section; an empty payload clears it; clearing a section removes all its items; additive upsert never removes items. [existing; `ReplaceTurnSectionAsync_ReplacesOnlyNamedSection`, `ReplaceTurnSectionAsync_EmptyPayload_ClearsSection`, `ClearTurnSectionAsync_RemovesAllItemsInSection`, `UpsertTurnAsync_StaysAdditive_DoesNotRemoveExistingItems`]\n187: - `ac-3` Item delete removes exactly the addressed item (string sections by value, commits by SHA, actions by order) and returns false for an unknown key. [existing; `DeleteTurnItemAsync_StringSection_RemovesByValue`, `DeleteTurnItemAsync_Commit_RemovesBySha`, `DeleteTurnItemAsync_Action_RemovesByOrder`, `DeleteTurnItemAsync_UnknownKey_ReturnsFalse`]\n188: - `ac-4` Replacing an unknown section throws `ArgumentException`. [existing; `ReplaceTurnSectionAsync_UnknownSection_Throws`, which asserts exactly this; the alias set in `ParseSection` (`SessionLogService.cs:1300-1317`) is existing behavior but no test asserts it today, so it is not claimed as an AC]\n189: - `ac-5` Sections `agentReview` and `hostileReview` (accepted case-insensitively as `agentreview`, `agent-review`, `agent_review`, `hostilereview`, `hostile-review`, `hostile_review`): replace sets the record (latest wins), clear resets it to the default status, item delete is rejected with 400. [S4 service, S5 REST/MCP/client]\n190: \n191: FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001 One turn per user request, with a server-issued request token. Every user message that reaches a host arrival mechanism (prompt hook, arrival bridge, or host-native hook adapter; D5, D6) maps to exactly one session-log turn whose `requestId` the server mints at ingress (operator D1).\n192: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC001 The plugin opens every turn through the server mint operation (`POST /qbrainai/sessionlog/{agent}/{sessionId}/mint` with an `ingressNonce`, or its client and REPL equivalents, called only by hooks and adapters); the server mints `req-<server UTC yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<8 lowercase hex random>`, opens the turn `in_progress`, and returns the token; the plugin never invents a requestId. [A1 server, A3 plugin]\n193: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC002 Repeating the mint with the same agent, MCP session id, and `ingressNonce` (network retry, two hook processes started for one event, or failsafe replay after an outage) returns the same requestId and opens no second turn. [A1 server, A3 plugin]\n194: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC003 Two user messages with identical prompt text (an intentional repeat, at any interval) are two events, derive two nonces, and receive two requestIds and two turns; two independent hook processes receiving one event (a doubly registered hook) derive one nonce from the host binding table and receive one requestId, for every hook host in the table (for copilot, D11 guarantees this by refusing to mint unless exactly one MCP prompt hook is registered). [A1 server, A3 plugin, A8 Node hosts]\n195: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC004 Distinct prompts open distinct turns. [A3]\n196: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC005 A message delivered while another turn is in progress (queued or mid-turn) gets its own turn, minted by the host's arrival mechanism (arrival bridge, prompt hook, or host-native hook adapter); the running turn is not changed. [A4]\n197: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC006 The Claude hook validator reports `duplicate` when an MCP hook script is registered by both `~/.claude/settings.json` and the enabled plugin, repairs it with a backup, and `-CheckOnly` never mutates settings. [A2]\n198: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC007 The hook installer adds no bridge hook for an event the enabled plugin already covers. [A2]\n199: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC008 When the server is unreachable at ingress, the hook persists a failsafe mint record carrying its nonce and opens a provisional local turn `pending-<nonce>`; on failsafe drain the server mints idempotently from the nonce and the plugin rebinds the provisional turn and every write bound to it to the minted requestId, in original order. [A3 +A5]\n200: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC009 Arrival bridge (D5): on claude-code, and on claude-cowork when P0.2 shows Cowork runs plugin hooks (otherwise FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-008-AC004 applies: Cowork reports `turn_logging_unsupported` and the feature matrix records the limitation), a message enqueued while a turn is running is minted from its transcript `enqueue` record before the model processes it, in state `queued`; the bridge mint and any later prompt-hook delivery of the same message yield one turn (the hook claims the bridge mint); the bridge resolves its host session per call, survives `/clear` and resume by restarting from SessionStart, and stops at SessionEnd. [A3]\n201: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC010 Queue lifecycle: a `queued` request becomes `delivered` (and the host session's write target) at `dequeue` with the prompt hook's claim or at `remove` with reason `absorbed_mid_turn`, and becomes `withdrawn` at `popAll` or a `remove` with any other or no reason; minting never moves the write target; a withdrawn request stays `in_progress` on the server until the model closes it (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC009). [A3]\n202: \n203: FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-002 Background and system events never open turns.\n204: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-002-AC001 Prompts that are `<task-notification>`, `<local-command-*>`, `<agent-message ...>` subagent hand-backs, or only system-reminder blocks, payloads marking a subagent (Claude Code `agent_id`, Grok `subagentType`), and deliveries with `MCP_SUBAGENT_ID`/`GROK_SUBAGENT_ID` set open no turn, call no mint, leave turn state unchanged, and report `system-event-skipped`; classification uses these structural markers only, never the topic or wording of the prompt. [A3 +A8]\n205: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-002-AC002 Slash-command (`<command-name>`) and `<user_query>` prompts open turns, and so does any user prompt that happens to contain a hostile-review or delegation brief (for example the brief that launches a Codex hostile reviewer, which needs its own reviewer turn). [A3 +A8]\n206: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-002-AC003 Hooks of subagents or parallel sessions sharing the workspace cache never change this session's turn state. [A4 +A8]\n207: \n208: FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-003 A new prompt never cancels an in-progress turn.\n209: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-003-AC001 Opening a turn never persists `canceled` for any other turn. [A4 +A8]\n210: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-003-AC002 The prior in-progress turn stays `in_progress` locally and on the server and remains addressable by requestId. [A4 +A8]\n211: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-003-AC003 Only the model closes turns; no hook calls complete or fail. [A7]\n212: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-003-AC004 The stop ledger lists every turn opened since the last passing stop. [A4]\n213: \n214: FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-004 Truthful persist outcomes.\n215: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-004-AC001 Session submit returns a per-turn outcome (`created`, `merged`, `skipped_terminal`, `skipped_duplicate`) with `storedStatus` and `reason` on REST, MCP `sessionlog_submit`, and the typed client. [A1]\n216: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-004-AC002 The plugin maps `skipped_terminal` to `persisted:false`, code `skipped_terminal`, non-zero exit, and a cleared failsafe record. [A5 +A8]\n217: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-004-AC003 Omitted-status enrichment and review-section writes on terminal turns report `merged` or success. [A1]\n218: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-004-AC004 A plugin verb targeting a locally terminal turn is refused with `turn_immutable` without a server call, except `recordReview` and requestClass-only updates. [A5 +A8]\n219: - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-004-AC005 A result from a server without per-turn outcomes is reported `outcomeVerified:false`. [A5]\n220: \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command '\"'$l=Get-Content -LiteralPath \"/home/sharpninja/.claude/plans/create-a-new-plan-giggly-music.md\"; for($i=1;$i -le 110 -and $i -le $l.Count;$i++){ \"{0,3}: {1}\" -f $i,$l[$i-1] }'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"  1: # Plan: Correct turn logging, request class, and agent/hostile reviews on session-log turns (BDPv4)\n  2: \n  3: Status: NOT APPROVED (operator, 2026-10-09). Do not implement any step, including P0, until the operator explicitly approves this plan. Persisted as MCP TODO `PLAN-SESSIONTURNREVIEW-001` (priority high); handoff `docs/handoffs/sessionturn-review-plan-20261009T022141Z.md`.\n  4: \n  5: Worktree `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/.claude/worktrees/session-20261009` (branch `worktree-session-20261009`). Source baseline: `f56dcf70` (== `origin/develop` on 2026-10-09, QBrainAi rename); every source anchor in this plan was verified against it. The branch has since gained only documentation and receipt commits (`git diff --name-only f56dcf70..HEAD` lists only `AGENTS.md`, removed handoff files `CODEX-HANDOFF.md`, `HANDOFF.md`, `docs/handoffs/*`, `docs/plans/handoff-*`, `docs/Project/Plugin-Simplification-Handoff.md`, plus `docs/setup/*` and `docs/receipts/*`; no `src/`, `tests/`, `build/`, or `plugins/` change). The approval HEAD is recorded by P0.1 (`git rev-parse HEAD` at approval) and P0.1 re-runs that diff to confirm the baseline still holds. Plugin repo `/home/sharpninja/github/mcpserver-claude-code-plugin` (1.118.0, `d3a07e0`). Plan request turn: `req-20261009T004308Z-prompt-e18e` in session `ClaudeCode-20261009T002615Z-plugin-session`.\n  6: \n  7: ## Context\n  8: \n  9: The operator requires an agent self-review and a hostile validation (HV) on every result, plus truthful, per-request audit in the MCP session log. Today:\n 10: \n 11: - Reviews have no structured home. They live in free-text turns, `docs/receipts/hv/*` (integer `accuracy`/`completeness` 0-100), and the unlinked HostileReview subsystem (`double` 0-1). No score in the codebase is a lossless decimal.\n 12: - Turn logging is not trustworthy, so anything attached to a turn would attach to the wrong request. Receipts observed in this session (plugin 1.118.0):\n 13:   - Twin turns per prompt (e.g. `req-20261009T011653Z-prompt-b776` and `-c9a3`): MCP hooks were then registered twice (`~/.claude/settings.json` bridge hooks plus the enabled plugin `mcpserver@mcpserver-local` `hooks/hooks.json`). The bridge hooks were removed later on 2026-10-09 (MEMORY-FACT-003); a read-only check at 2026-10-09T17:54Z found no `hooks` key in `~/.claude/settings.json` or either `settings.local.json`, one plugin install, and one registration per event. The defect that let the duplicate go unreported remains: `validate-claude-mcp-hooks.ps1` wraps the `enabledPlugins` map as one item (line ~72), so it never sees the plugin as enabled and would report `valid` for a duplicate. A2 is therefore a regression fix verified against a fresh live inventory, not a cleanup of a current duplicate. Observed later the same day with hooks active: the prompt hook opens phantom turns for background task notifications, and each such open supersede-cancels the real turn in progress (`req-20261009T175347Z-prompt-dc63`, `req-20261009T175421Z-prompt-79dd`).\n 14:   - A new prompt cancels the in-progress turn: `Invoke-ReplSupersedeCurrentTurnIfInProgress` (`plugins/core/lib-ps/repl-invoke.ps1:2131`, called at `:2300`). Canceled while being worked: `6067`, `5360`, `bc34`, `8ab5`, `11dd`, `ea25`, `9a7d`, and the plan request `e18e`.\n 15:   - Background `<task-notification>` events open turns (`-986f`, `-06ab`, `-290b`, `-4ced`, `-695f`).\n 16:   - False durable-write receipt: `appendActions` on `req-20261009T005031Z-prompt-d3a5` returned `code: persisted, persisted: true`, but the server turn has 0 actions. The submit terminal guard `continue`s silently (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/SessionLogService.cs:1606-1618`) while the controller hard-codes `persisted = true` (`SessionLogController.cs:71-86`).\n 17:   - The stop gate auto-closes turns as `'Auto-closed by PowerShell stop gate.'` (`plugins/core/lib-ps/plugin-hook.ps1:1017`).\n 18:   - A single shared `current-turn.yaml` couples all turn edits, so edits to one request land on another.\n 19:   - Cross-session interference: this session's UserPromptSubmit hook reported `req-20261009T012640Z-prompt-b2ff` (a turn of a different ClaudeCode session, likely the Plan subagent's prompt) as \"the root work turn\"; hooks of subagents and parallel sessions share the workspace cache.\n 20:   - Hook-current turns can exist only locally (`req-20261009T013814Z-prompt-7ee0`: server PATCH returned \"planFile is omitted\", turn absent server-side).\n 21: \n 22: Intended outcome: every user request is exactly one turn whose edits are independent of every other turn; every write receipt is truthful; every turn carries a `requestClass` and two review records (`agentReview`, `hostileReview`) with lossless decimal scores, a status lifecycle, and configurable pass thresholds; the server enforces completion rules; FR/TR/TEST/AC/UC are complete and traceable.\n 23: \n 24: ## Locked operator decisions (2026-10-09)\n 25: \n 26: Turn logging\n 27: - Every user request (including messages queued mid-turn) is exactly one turn; actions and decisions map to the turn of the request that caused them, determined by the write-attribution rule below (D2). Enforcement of turn-logging correctness is Phase A and ships first.\n 28: - Plugin turn edits are independent: opening, editing, closing, or canceling one turn never changes another turn's local or server state; concurrent edits to different turns do not interfere.\n 29: - Request identity (operator D1, 2026-10-09: \"have the server issue a request token when a message arrives\"): the server mints every turn's `requestId` when a user message arrives (ingress), through the new mint operation `POST /qbrainai/sessionlog/{agent}/{sessionId}/mint`. The mint call carries an idempotency nonce derived from the host's own identity for that message event (host binding table): a host per-prompt id, or a SHA-256 of host event fields that every delivery of that event carries identically. Every delivery of one event therefore sends the same nonce and receives the same token, including two hook processes started by a doubly registered hook, network retries, and failsafe replay; two separate user messages (including two intentionally identical prompts) are two events and receive two tokens. The host id is only the idempotency key; the turn identity is the server-issued token. There is no prompt-text dedupe window. Every mint comes from a host arrival mechanism (a prompt hook, the arrival bridge, or a host-native hook adapter); no supported host relies on the model to mint. The stop gate's `duplicate_delivery_suspected` warning is a diagnostic only and is never counted as D1 compliance.\n 30: - Queued-message arrival (operator D5, 2026-10-09: \"Arrival bridge\"): a message the operator sends while a turn is running is minted when it is enqueued, not when the model later processes it. On hosts that record the enqueue in a transcript (Claude Code), a per-host-session arrival bridge started by the SessionStart hook tails the transcript and follows each queued message through the lifecycle the transcript records. Observed in this workspace's Claude Code transcripts on 2026-10-09: 117 `enqueue`, 79 `dequeue`, 35 `remove` (27 with reason `absorbed_mid_turn`), 2 `popAll`; a normal prompt is `enqueue` then `dequeue` within milliseconds, a mid-turn message is `enqueue` then `remove` with `absorbed_mid_turn` when it is injected into the running turn (for example the operator's mid-turn message beginning \"write a global memory with the\" was removed with `absorbed_mid_turn` at 2026-10-09T14:35:47.730Z), and `popAll` pulls queued text back into the input box. The bridge mints at `enqueue` (state `queued`); the request becomes `delivered` at `dequeue` (the prompt hook then claims the mint) or at `remove` with `absorbed_mid_turn`; it becomes `withdrawn` at `popAll` or a `remove` with any other or no reason. Task notifications and other system events that the host also enqueues are classified and never minted.\n 31: - Hosts without an arrival mechanism (operator D6, 2026-10-09: \"Adapters or unsupported\"): every host gets an arrival adapter wherever the host offers any mechanism (prompt hook, transcript enqueue record, host-native hook, message-queue event). A host with no arrival mechanism is declared unsupported for turn logging until it gains one: its plugin reports `turn_logging_unsupported` on every session-log verb and writes nothing, and no first-sight minting is used anywhere.\n 32: - Plugin equivalence (operator D7, 2026-10-09, confirmed in chat \"1 yes\" for the requirement and its qualification posted by Codex in `MEMORY-PROCESS-007` at 17:48:48Z and 17:52:06Z): all QBrain.AI plugins keep equivalent functionality; shared capabilities, contracts, fixes, and behavior changes go to every plugin; host-specific adaptations preserve equivalent supported behavior; equivalence is limited by what each host's plugin infrastructure can support, no extraordinary effort is spent forcing unworkable equivalence, and each such limit is documented as a feature-specific capability limitation instead of being claimed as equivalent. A D6 `turn_logging_unsupported` host is such a documented limitation. The limitations live in `docs/AGENT-PLUGIN-FEATURE-MATRIX.md` (one row per plugin and feature: supported, adapted, or limited, with the reason and the receipt), updated in A9 and S11 and checked by a docs contract test.\n 33: - Ninth plugin (operator D8, 2026-10-09: \"2 yes\"): `mcpserver-grok-bot-plugin` is in scope like the other eight.\n 34: - Write attribution (operator D2, 2026-10-09: tie each write to its request automatically through the agent app's hooks): every verb invocation is bound to one request automatically from host context, without the model naming it: the binding is the write target of the invoking host session at the moment the invocation starts, captured once and stored immutably with that invocation (its persist record and failsafe record), so a later message never retargets an invocation already started. A host session's write target is the request most recently delivered to the model in that host session: a prompt the host hands to the model as a new turn (prompt hook, including its claim of a bridge mint at `dequeue`), or a queued message absorbed into the running turn (`absorbed_mid_turn`). Minting alone never moves the write target, so work the model is still doing for request R1 after R2 is enqueued stays on R1 until R2 is delivered. Explicit `requestId` remains accepted as an override, for example to continue R1 work after R2 was absorbed, and it is required while two or more delivered requests are open in the host session (D9). Attribution therefore maps each write to the request most recently delivered to the model in its host session when the write started. A write is never attributed by guesswork: when a verb cannot read its host session key from its own invocation context (host binding table), it binds only if exactly one live host session of its agent exists in the workspace (warning `host_session_inferred`); with none it is refused (`host_session_unknown`), and with more than one it is refused (`host_session_ambiguous`), in both cases unless it carries an explicit `requestId`. No verb ever targets the workspace-level `current-turn.yaml`.\n 35: - Ambiguous attribution (operator D9, 2026-10-09: \"Refuse when ambiguous\"): no host supplies a per-action signal of which request caused a write, so automatic binding applies only while exactly one delivered request is open in the host session. While two or more delivered requests are open (for example R2 absorbed mid-turn while R1 is still open), a write without an explicit `requestId` is refused with `request_ambiguous`, naming the open requests; the model passes `requestId` until all but one are closed. Writes are never attributed by guesswork.\n 36: - Copilot duplicate deliveries (operator D11, 2026-10-09: \"Supported only if single hook\"): Copilot gives no stable per-prompt id and separately registered hooks may see different `timestamp` values, so Copilot turn logging is supported only while the plugin's duplicate check proves exactly one MCP prompt hook across all Copilot hook sources; otherwise the Copilot prompt hook reports `turn_logging_unsupported` before any mint, and the condition is a D7 documented limitation until fixed. One Copilot prompt therefore never becomes two turns.\n 37: - Node runtime floor (operator D12, 2026-10-09: \"Keep Node 18/20\"): the Node core and the TypeScript hosts keep their declared engines (`plugins/core/lib-node/package.json` `node >=20`; cline, cline-v2, opencode `>=18`). Exact review scores are read with a raw-token parser in the Node core, not with `JSON.parse` reviver `context.source` (absent on Node 20, V8 11.3), and the parser is tested on Node 18, 20, 22, and the latest release.\n 38: \n 39: Request class\n 40: - Turn scalar `requestClass`: `Code` | `Docs` | `Chore` (case-insensitive in, canonical PascalCase out, anything else rejected). Required, enforced at transition to `completed` or `failed`, not at creation (hooks create turns before the agent can classify). Legacy rows backfilled to `Unclassified` (readable; never accepted on new writes from agent surfaces).\n 41: \n 42: Review record (shared by `agentReview` and `hostileReview`)\n 43: - Fields: `status` (`Unnecessary` | `Unstarted` | `Running` | `Pass` | `Fail` | `Other`), `otherNotes`, `correctness`, `completeness` (lossless decimal 0..1), `correctnessThreshold`, `completenessThreshold` (server-stamped, read-only), `reasons` (string[]), `reviewer`, `reviewedAt` (UTC), `requestJsonlPath`, `responseJsonlPath`. No `passed` bool.\n 44: - One record per kind per turn; latest write wins.\n 45: - Server creates both at turn creation as `Unstarted`. `hostileReview` flips to `Unnecessary` when `requestClass` becomes `Chore`, and back to `Unstarted` if the class changes away while still `Unnecessary`. Records past `Unstarted`/`Unnecessary` are never auto-changed. Legacy turns are backfilled `agentReview=Unstarted`, `hostileReview=Unstarted`.\n 46: - `otherNotes` with text is rejected unless `status` is `Other`.\n 47: - Strict per-status fields: `Unnecessary`/`Unstarted` carry no scores, reasons, reviewer, timestamps, or paths; `Running` requires `reviewer`, forbids scores; `Pass`/`Fail` require `correctness`, `completeness`, `reviewer`, `reviewedAt`; `hostileReview` `Pass`/`Fail` also require `requestJsonlPath` and `responseJsonlPath`; `Other` requires non-empty `otherNotes` (the Other reason; operator D4, 2026-10-09: \"Other requires Other Reason be populated\") and allows optional scores, `reasons`, `reviewer`, `reviewedAt`, and receipt paths. `Unnecessary` is server-managed only.\n 48: - Thresholds: `Mcp:SessionLog:ReviewThresholds:{Agent|Hostile}:{Correctness|Completeness}` (decimal 0..1, per workspace, per-instance override), default 0.98, inclusive (score >= threshold passes). Pass requires both scores to meet their thresholds. The server derives `Pass`/`Fail`; a supplied `Pass`/`Fail` that contradicts the derivation is rejected (422 `review_status_conflict`); omitting status lets the server derive it. `Other` is never derived. Thresholds in force are stamped on the record.\n 49: - Names stay `correctness`/`completeness`. HV tooling maps `accuracy` -> `correctness` and integer percent -> decimal/100.\n 50: \n 51: Completion rules\n 52: - `completed` requires `requestClass` plus `agentReview.status` in `Pass`/`Fail`/`Other`; for `Code`/`Docs` also `hostileReview.status` in `Pass`/`Fail`/`Other`; for `Chore` `hostileReview` is not checked (HV not required). `Unstarted`/`Running` block where checked. Pass is not required (Fail/Other complete).\n 53: - `failed` requires `requestClass` only. Reviews remain writable on terminal turns.\n 54: - Server-internal writers (QuadBrain chat, AuditedAgentCliClient, WorkspacePolicyService, federation replication, transcript/session ingestion) are exempt by code-path origin; they never fabricate reviews.\n 55: \n 56: Scope and process\n 57: - Server + client + REPL + MCP tools + plugin core, and every agent plugin updated and tested as needed (matrix below).\n 58: - Strict BDPv4 per slice (below). Complete FR/TR/TEST with ACs and Use Cases (UC) for every new FR.\n 59: - Hostile validation runs on Codex CLI with model `gpt-6-sol` at reasoning effort `xhigh`, launched by the implementing agent at the end of every slice once all of that slice's gates are green (operator D10, 2026-10-09: \"Only HV when all green at end of slice\"), and at P0's end (HV-P0); the closing slices A9 and S11 also carry the phase-wide HV-A and HV-B. This replaces the earlier PR-comment HV protocol (operator, 2026-10-09: \"Yes, replace the PR-comment HV with Codex gpt-6-sol xhigh\").\n 60: \n 61: ## Agent plugin matrix (all plugins updated and tested)\n 62: \n 63: Nine repos: the eight listed in `build/Build.SyncAgentPlugins.cs:19-28` plus `mcpserver-grok-bot-plugin` (D8; A9 adds it to that list; see also `docs/AGENT-PLUGIN-AVAILABILITY.md` and `docs/AGENT-PLUGIN-FEATURE-MATRIX.md`). State re-verified 2026-10-09T18:48Z by a read-only inventory (`git rev-parse`, `git status --porcelain`, `sha256sum lib/plugin-hook.ps1`, `CORE-MANIFEST.yaml`, `git ls-remote --symref`): all nine are checked out under `/home/sharpninja/github`; remote default branches are `main` for eight and `master` for opencode; six are on their default branch at 1.118.0 with `lib/plugin-hook.ps1` SHA-256 prefix `e4cd6116`, except grok-bot, whose file hashes to `d917feb6` although its `CORE-MANIFEST.yaml:14` declares `e4cd6116` (core drift that A9's sync must resolve and its receipt must show fixed); codex, grok, and cline are detached at 1.108.0 with the older core `26137529`. The shared core today invents `req-<ts>-prompt-<rand4>` ids (`plugin-hook.ps1:865`), keeps one turn pointer per workspace and agent key (`resolve-cache-dir.ps1:64-68`, `repl-invoke.ps1:122,817`), reads the undocumented env `CLAUDE_STOP_HOOK_ACTIVE` (`plugin-hook.ps1:944`) instead of the payload `stop_hook_active`, and looks for `CLAUDE_SESSION_ID` (`agent-runtime-header.ps1:165-169`) although Claude Code sets `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`.\n 64: - PowerShell hosts (receive `plugins/core/lib-ps` via `sync-plugin-core.ps1` and host wrappers):\n 65:   - `mcpserver-claude-code-plugin`: remote default `main` `d3a07e0`; local on `main` at `d3a07e0`, 2 uncommitted files, 1.118.0, hook `e4cd6116`. `hooks/hooks.json`: SessionStart, SessionEnd, PreCompact, PostCompact, UserPromptSubmit, Stop, PostToolUse (ExitPlanMode, Write|Edit); no PreToolUse or SubagentStop. Pester + bats (CI `bats tests/`). Its `.codex-plugin/plugin.json:21-54` uses event names Codex does not define (`sessionStart`, `taskComplete`, ...).\n 66:   - `mcpserver-claude-cowork-plugin`: local on `main` at `c344369` (remote default `main`), 2 uncommitted files, 1.118.0, hook `e4cd6116`. Same hook set as Claude Code with `${COWORK_PLUGIN_ROOT:-${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}}`; `.mcp.json` points at `bin/mcpserver-stdio.cmd`, which does not exist in the repo. Pester + bats.\n 67:   - `mcpserver-codex-plugin`: remote `main` `ce4a7a64`; local detached at `b776df5`, 11 uncommitted files, 1.108.0, hook `26137529`. Registers no hooks today (no `hooks/hooks.json`, no manifest `hooks`); turn logging is manual through `skills/enforcement/SKILL.md:12-69`. Pester + bats.\n 68:   - `mcpserver-copilot-plugin`: local on `main` at `ff622fa` (remote default `main`), 2 uncommitted files, 1.118.0, hook `e4cd6116`. `plugin.json:47-49` points at a root `