116`. Exports `createMcpServerPlugin` (`src/index.ts:1`); hooks beforeRun, beforeModel, beforeTool, afterTool, afterRun (`src/plugin.ts:211-291`); the vendored core invents session and request ids, closes the session after every run, and reads a `prompt` field Cline does not send; it pins `@cline/core ^0.0.41`, while the context fields used below (`conversationId`, `runId`, `toolCallId`, `snapshot.messages`) are verified in `@cline/shared` 0.0.92. Installed under `~/.cline/plugins/_installed/local/mcpserver-cline-v2-plugin-*` (`Build.SyncAgentPlugins.cs:788-797`); host fork `/home/sharpninja/github/cline`. jest (`npm test -- --runInBand`).\n 74:   - `mcpserver-opencode-plugin`: local on `master` at `0c9abc2` (remote default `master`), clean, 1.118.0, hook `e4cd6116`. Exports `createMcpServerPlugin` (also default; `src/index.ts:1`, `src/plugin.ts:148,258`), not `createQBrainAiPlugin`. Hooks `event`, `chat.message`, `tool.execute.before/after` (`plugin.ts:196-255`); its start regex (`plugin.ts:136-142`) matches no documented OpenCode event, so turn logging never starts, and `session.idle` (the real turn end) is unhandled; the factory treats OpenCode's plugin input as its own config (`plugin.ts:148`). jest via `npm test` (`node --experimental-vm-modules`, `package.json:16`).\n 75: - All nine were present locally at 2026-10-09T18:48Z; a repo missing at P0 time is acquired via the marker tool-registry route (`/mcpserver/tools/search?keyword=<name>`, install from the `official` bucket, run the returned `commandTemplate` with `targetParent=/home/sharpninja/github`). This is the marker's documented bootstrap exception to the plugin-only rule (`AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` lines 196 and 204: plugin acquisition through the authenticated tool-registry endpoints); it is not used for session, TODO, requirements, or traceability operations.\n 76: \n 77: Host binding table (locks the D1 nonce source, the D5 arrival source, and the D2 host session key per host; every turn token is still minted by the server). Rules common to all hosts:\n 78: - Nonce: computed from the event itself, never from the hook process, so every delivery of one event yields the same nonce; a value outside `^[A-Za-z0-9._:-]{1,64}$` is replaced by the 64 lowercase hex characters of its SHA-256.\n 79: - Host session key: read from the verb's own invocation context as listed per host. Long-lived processes (stdio MCP servers, the arrival bridge itself) never trust a key captured at spawn time, because a host may change the session in place (Claude Code `/clear`; resume without an explicit id keeps the startup id in long-lived subprocesses per the Claude Code env-var reference); they resolve the key per call by the live-session rule.\n 80: - Queued-message arrival on every host (D5, D6): P0.2 records, per host, whether a message sent while a turn is running reaches an arrival mechanism at enqueue. Where the prompt event itself fires at enqueue, it is the arrival mechanism; where it fires only at processing time, the host's transcript or queue event is used through an arrival adapter with the same `queued`/`delivered`/`withdrawn` lifecycle as the Claude Code bridge; where neither exists, the host is unsupported for turn logging (`turn_logging_unsupported`).\n 81: - Live-session rule (D2 fallback): a host session is live from its first mint until its session-end event (Claude Code `SessionEnd`, including reason `clear`; Codex `SessionEnd`; Copilot session end; cline-v2 and opencode session close) marks `sessions/<key>/ended.yaml`, or until 12 hours without a mint or verb. A verb without its own key binds to the single live session of its agent in the workspace (`host_session_inferred`), and is refused with `host_session_unknown` (none live) or `host_session_ambiguous` (several live) unless it passes an explicit `requestId`.\n 82: - claude-code: nonce payload `prompt_id`; hook key payload `session_id`; verb key env `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` read at verb start in shell, PowerShell, and hook subprocesses (equals `session_id`; those subprocesses see the updated value after `/clear`); stdio MCP verbs use the live-session rule; payloads carrying `agent_id` are subagent events (system events, no turn); the subagent's own Bash and PowerShell verbs carry the parent's `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` and bind to the parent's write target; stop uses payload `stop_hook_active`. Arrival bridge (D5): started by SessionStart with the payload `transcript_path`; it processes `queue-operation` lines in file order. `enqueue` whose `content` the system-event classifier accepts as a user request: mint with nonce `q-` + SHA-256(`session_id` LF enqueue `timestamp` LF `content`) truncated to 62 hex characters, add `{ requestId, nonce, enqueueOffset, enqueueTimestamp, promptSha256, state: queued }` to the queue in `sessions/<key>/turn-state.yaml`. `dequeue` (no content): no state change by itself; the prompt hook that fires for that prompt, under `turn-open.lock`, claims the oldest `queued` entry whose `promptSha256` equals its prompt's hash, sets it `delivered`, and makes it the write target (it mints with `prompt_id` only when no entry matches). `remove` with reason `absorbed_mid_turn`: the oldest `queued` entry with that `content` hash becomes `delivered` and the write target. `remove` with any other or no reason, and `popAll` (each content line it carries): the matching `queued` entry becomes `withdrawn`. Any other operation or an unrecognized shape: warning `arrival_bridge_format_unrecognized`, no state change. Each record's state change and the advance of `appliedThroughOffset` past it are one atomic replace of `turn-state.yaml` (temp file, flush, rename, under `turn-open.lock`), and records that start below `appliedThroughOffset` are skipped, so a restart never re-applies `enqueue`, `remove`, or `popAll`; the server mint for `enqueue` happens before that replace and is idempotent (same nonce, `merged`). A `remove` or `popAll` targets the oldest `queued` entry whose `promptSha256` matches and whose `enqueueOffset` is below the record's offset.\n 83: - claude-cowork: same fields and arrival bridge as claude-code. P0.2 verifies that Cowork executes plugin hooks; if it does not, Cowork has no arrival mechanism and is unsupported for turn logging under D6 (`turn_logging_unsupported`, nothing written) until it gains one. A9 repairs the dangling `bin/mcpserver-stdio.cmd` connector.\n 84: - codex: A9 adds `hooks/hooks.json` (SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, PostToolUse, Stop, SessionEnd per the Codex hooks reference) and keeps the manual enforcement steps in `skills/enforcement/SKILL.md` until the hook trust check below passes; nonce payload `turn_id`; hook key payload `session_id` (subagents share the parent's); verb key env `CODEX_THREAD_ID` only if P0.2 proves it equals `session_id` in shell commands, else the live-session rule. Codex skips plugin hooks until the operator trusts that hook definition (OpenAI Codex plugin documentation), so A9 and the rollout include an operator trust step and a live assertion that a fresh Codex prompt produces a hook-minted server turn.\n 85: - copilot: A9 points `plugin.json` at `hooks/hooks.json` and removes the dangling `.sh` hook file; nonce `cp-` + SHA-256(`sessionId` LF `timestamp` LF `prompt`) truncated to 61 hex characters, from the UserPromptSubmit payload fields the Copilot CLI hooks reference documents (`sessionId`, `timestamp` in Unix milliseconds, `cwd`, `prompt`; VS Code form `session_id`, ISO `timestamp`); hook key `sessionId`/`session_id`; verbs use the live-session rule (no session env var is documented). The reference does not promise that two separately registered hook commands receive an identical `timestamp`, so the Copilot adoption also enforces a single delivery boundary: an A2-equivalent check enumerates every source the Copilot hooks reference documents (policy `/etc/github-copilot/policy.d/*.json`; repository `.github/hooks/*.json`; user `~/.copilot/hooks/` honoring `COPILOT_HOME`; inline hooks in `.github/copilot/settings.json`, `.github/copilot/settings.local.json`, and the cross-tool `.claude/settings.json` and `.claude/settings.local.json`; `~/.copilot/settings.json`; plugin `hooks.json` or `hooks/hooks.json`; all sources run, and the Copilot cloud agent reads only `.github/hooks/*.json`), detects duplicate MCP prompt hooks across all of them, repairs the duplicates it owns (plugin, repository, and user files, with a backup), and for a duplicate it cannot remove (a policy file) fails closed: Copilot turn logging reports `turn_logging_unsupported` with operator remediation text, recorded as a D7 documented limitation; P0.2 captures two real deliveries of one prompt from two registered hooks when the Copilot CLI is available and records whether their `timestamp` values match (evidence only; D11 does not depend on it); A9 tests duplicate detection with one fixture per source, that with exactly one MCP prompt hook a delivery mints one turn, and that with two MCP prompt hooks registered (in any pair of sources, including two deliveries that differ only in `timestamp`) the prompt hook reports `turn_logging_unsupported` before any mint and the server receives zero mints (D11).\n 86: - grok: the core reads camelCase keys when the host is grok: nonce `promptId`, hook key `sessionId`, stop `stopHookActive`, subagent `subagentType`; verb key env `GROK_SESSION_ID` only if P0.2 proves it is set for shell commands, else the live-session rule. Grok's default execution of `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks is a duplicate-registration source that A2's validator reports; with `promptId` as nonce, such duplicates still mint one turn.\n 87: - grok-bot: same payload handling as grok (camelCase `promptId` nonce, `sessionId` hook key, `stopHookActive`, `subagentType`) until P0.2 records the Grok Bot cloud runtime's actual hook payload, session environment for shell commands, and queued-message behavior; each P0.2 outcome selects among the rules above (prompt