ical).** Early on 2026-10-09 MCP hooks were registered twice, by the `~/.claude/settings.json` bridge and by the enabled plugin `hooks/hooks.json`. The bridge hooks were removed later that day (MEMORY-FACT-003); at 17:54Z `~/.claude/settings.json` had no `hooks` key and the plugin registered one hook per event. Current defect instead: the prompt hook opens phantom turns for background task notifications, and each such open supersede-cancels the real turn in progress (for example `req-20261009T175347Z-prompt-dc63`).\n- **New prompts cancel open turns.** `Invoke-ReplSupersedeCurrentTurnIfInProgress` cancels the in-progress turn when the next prompt arrives.\n- **Phantom turns.** Background `<task-notification>` events open turns. Cancel them with a mapping-correction decision; no work belongs there.\n- **Unreliable turn pointer.** `.mcpServer/claude/current-turn.yaml` can point at a phantom or canceled turn. Target turns by explicit requestId with `client.SessionLog.BeginTurnAsync`, `PatchTurnAsync`, and `CompleteTurnAsync`, invoked through the plugin `lib/repl-invoke.ps1`. Verify every write with `client.SessionLog.QueryAsync`. A write to a terminal turn can report `persisted` while storing nothing.\n- **Cache location.** The plugin cache dir follows `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`, otherwise the marker walk-up. Set it to the worktree when you run the wrapper manually.\n- **Large payloads fail.** `Invoke-McpPlugin.ps1` passes params as a command-line argument, so payloads above roughly 128 KB fail on Linux. Invoke `lib/repl-invoke.ps1` in-process with `&` instead.\n- **Partial requirements ingest.** It is non-atomic per kind and can apply part of a payload before failing with \"The change could not be saved.\" Ingest per kind and only missing entries.\n\n## Next steps (when the operator resumes)\n\n1. Run session start per `CLAUDE.md`: `/add-profile`, marker signature, `/health` nonce, plugin bootstrap, history and TODOs, then an initial turn.\n2. Read TODO `PLAN-SESSIONTURNREVIEW-001` and present the plan to the operator for corrections or approval. Plan-readiness hostile validation on Codex gpt-6-sol xhigh is iterated to AGREE >= 98/98 before approval is requested (operator, 2026-10-09). Round 1: `docs/receipts/hv/hostile-validator-plan-readiness-20261009T145521Z.md` (DISAGREE 84/68, 16 findings; four operator decisions D1-D4 answered and applied with the twelve implementer fixes). Round 2: `docs/receipts/hv/hostile-validator-plan-readiness-r2-20261009T160546Z.md` (DISAGREE 90/78, findings R2-01..R2-10; operator decision D5 answered R2-08; the operator then directed a full self-analysis of the plan before the next round). Round 3: `docs/receipts/hv/hostile-validator-plan-readiness-r3-20261009T164531Z.md` (DISAGREE 90/82, 1 PASS, 7 FAIL, 2 UNKNOWN, findings R3-01..R3-08; operator decision D6 \"Adapters or unsupported\" answered R3-03). Round 4: `docs/receipts/hv/hostile-validator-plan-readiness-r4-20261009T174055Z.md` (DISAGREE 88/76, 2 PASS, 8 FAIL; findings F-R4-01..F-R4-07; operator decision D9 answered F-R4-04). Round 5: `docs/receipts/hv/hostile-validator-plan-readiness-r5-20261009T181414Z.md` (DISAGREE 87/80, 2 PASS, 8 FAIL; findings F-R5-01..F-R5-09; operator decision D10 answered F-R5-05; the requirement ids are now FR-SUPPORT-016, TR-SUPPORT-CORE-016, TEST-SUPPORT-023 because `010G` fails the id grammar). Round 6: `docs/receipts/hv/hostile-validator-plan-readiness-r6-20261009T185109Z.md` (DISAGREE 92/88, 3 PASS, 7 FAIL; findings F-R6-01..F-R6-08, all implementer edits; the operator confirmed the D10 interpretation with \"agree\"). Round 7: `docs/receipts/hv/hostile-validator-plan-readiness-r7-20261009T191539Z.md` (DISAGREE 96/94; findings F-R7-01..F-R7-05; operator decisions D11 \"Supported only if single hook\" for Copilot and D12 \"Keep Node 18/20\" with a raw-token score parser answered F-R7-03 and F-R7-04). Round 8 was launched at 2026-10-09T19:42:42Z and stopped by the implementer before any verdict, because the operator asked for another holistic self-review first (\"Before next HV, do another holistic self-review. Have next HV do holistic review.\"); its partial receipts `docs/receipts/hv/20261009T194242Z-plan-readiness-r8.request.jsonl` and `.response.jsonl` are kept as an aborted run, not a verdict. The self-review fixed: D2 and FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-007-AC002 now state that the explicit `requestId` is required under D9 (`request_ambiguous`) and name `request_unknown`; C9 lists every typed code; C1 uses full ids; the coordination section records the partial clearance and the 19:51:42Z NOTICE; the HV protocol, UC-7, and UC-1 follow D10, D6, D9, and D11; FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001 covers every arrival mechanism; AC records are in numeric order. The next round is a holistic review of the whole plan. Coordination with Codex's QB-AGENT-001 (same `plugins/core/lib-node` and server session-log files) runs through Global memory `MEMORY-PROCESS-007`; the plan's section \"Coordination with QB-AGENT-001\" holds contracts C1-C12. Later rounds add receipts with the same gate prefix.\n3. Apply operator corrections to the plan file and the TODO description, then re-verify the round trip.\n4. Only after explicit approval, execute P0 in order (P0.1-P0.9). Stop at HV-P0, which runs on Codex gpt-6-sol xhigh.\n5. Triage is filed (nothing left to file; one group relocation pending, below). The plan's \"Risks and incidental bugs\" \"To file\" list was verified against source and filed on 2026-10-09: `triage-report-41d7d27c53f2464f8339fe963079a55e` (gated SessionQuery omits commit files), gated restore and clone do not carry PlanFile/TodoId (the original `-53dc4c8b359d496ea5327841deb1ea45` overstated the effect and was withdrawn by soft-deleting its one-report group; the corrected replacement's group `triage-group-804b21086f3019ab` was filed with `workspacePath` set to the worktree `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/.claude/worktrees/session-20261009` by mistake; a read-only `workflow.triage.getGroup` under the worktree path at 2026-10-09T18:09Z returned it (title \"Gated restore and clone do not carry PlanFile and TodoId\", 1 report with status `grouped`, group status `failed`), after the round-3 reviewer had seen not found under both paths; relocation to the main workspace is still pending, and the `failed` group status is not processed triage), `-953f44d7574942419e6d5d3123acb971` (revive resurrects replaced children), `-d8c40d80a4ba454e8b5b96b9b5762105` (restamp skips commit files), `-ab06ac3befed4ff99725ef6047715158` (federation snapshot drops turn fields), `-04659db176a8473bbf2aa9b6caf1f98a` (`filesModified` vs `FileModified`). Also filed: `-36d2da231add47f18af5abd35a340e5f` (dialog ordinals repeat; delete-by-ordinal removes several items), `-ed3b0381bd2948e58d6315f6e1601b02` (delete-item fails for values containing `/`), `-bd1f438638f64f808e53a407aeb58c98` (hook validator depends on `CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT`), `-e1f01430477f441fb86538469d15fc15` (plugin memory verbs log an undocumented `memory` action type and reuse action orders), and `-02e7a0198d064403a85b13efa6273637` (dangling `CODEX-HANDOFF.md` citations). The plan's risks section now lists these filed ids instead of \"To file\" (round-2 edit, 2026-10-09). Moving group `triage-group-804b21086f3019ab` to the main workspace (operator: \"2 move\") is still pending.\n\n## Do not\n\n- Implement anything before approval.\n- Edit `docs/Project/TODO.yaml`, session-log storage, or requirements storage directly. Use the plugin wrappers.\n- Run a wiki-mode requirements import (it deletes records).\n- Deploy without `./build.ps1 UpdateService` and operator approval.\n- Drop or apply the preserved stashes without operator direction.\n- Move any TODO to a done state without HV AGREE >= 98/98.\n\n## Handoff hygiene\n\nThe operator directed removal of every existing handoff in the worktree. The first attempt was blocked by the Claude Code auto-mode classifier. On 2026-10-09T03:12Z, at the operator's explicit \"Remove them\", ClaudeCode removed all 12 with `git rm`; the deletions are committed in `3ed6c639` and pushed. All 12 files are tracked at `f56dcf70` and remain readable with `git show f56dcf70:<path>`. The dangling `CODEX-HANDOFF.md` citations in `docs/Byrd-Dev-Process-v4-Project-Management-Guide.md` (lines 6 and 926) are filed as `triage-report-02e7a0198d064403a85b13efa6273637`.\n\n- `HANDOFF.md` (2026-06-23 Grok)\n- `CODEX-HANDOFF.md` (2026-06-25 Codex)\n- `docs/handoffs/grok-completion-program-20260909.md`\n- `docs/handoffs/handback-overlay-from-quadbrain-qbagent-20260910.md`\n- `docs/handoffs/handoff-qbexec-mcp-tools-compaction-20260910.md`\n- `docs/handoffs/integrate-session-start-opensession-20260916.md`\n- `docs/handoffs/sessionlife-p2-contracts-pr72-20261001T113858Z.md` (stale: PR #72 merged 2026-10-03T09:39:23Z)\n- `docs/handoffs/txnkeyserver-all-adapters-20260919.md` (already marked superseded)\n- `docs/plans/handoff-agent-parity-plan-2026-05-28.yaml`\n- `docs/plans/handoff-mcpserver-503-auth-readiness-2026-06-15.md`\n- `docs/plans/handoff-parity-v4-phase1-complete-2026-05-29.md`\n- `docs/Project/Plugin-Simplification-Handoff.md`\n\nKept on purpose:\n- `docs/handoffs/example.md`: the handoff-ingestion worked sample, referenced by `docs/Handoff-Ingestion.md`, `docs/CLIENT-INTEGRATION.md`, the REPL guides, `plugins/core/skills/handoff/SKILL.md`, and `HandoffSkillDelegationTests.cs`.\n- `docs/Handoff-Ingestion.md` and its wiki copies: feature documentation, not handoffs.\n\nThis file is the only active handoff for this workstream.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command '\"'$f=Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath \"docs/receipts/hv\" -File -Filter \"hostile-validator-plan-readiness-*.md\" | Sort-Object Name; $f | ForEach-Object { \"=== $($_.Name) lines=$((Get-Content -LiteralPath $_.FullName).Count) ===\"; Select-String -LiteralPath $_.FullName -Pattern \"OverallVerdict|Accuracy:|Completeness:|F-R[1-7]-|R[1-3]-0|''^## .*finding|''^### .*finding\" | Select-Object -First 30 | ForEach-Object { $_.Line } }'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command '\"'$f=Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath \"docs/receipts/hv\" -File -Filter \"hostile-validator-plan-readiness-*.md\" | Sort-Object Name; $f | ForEach-Object { \"=== $($_.Name) lines=$((Get-Content -LiteralPath $_.FullName).Count) ===\"; Select-String -LiteralPath $_.FullName -Pattern \"OverallVerdict|Accuracy:|Completeness:|F-R[1-7]-|R[1-3]-0|''^## .*finding|''^### .*finding\" | Select-Object -First 30 | ForEach-Object { $_.Line } }'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"=== hostile-validator-plan-readiness-20261009T145521Z.md lines=70 ===\nOverallVerdict: DISAGREE. Accuracy: 84/100. Completeness: 68/100. Confidence: 93/100. Confirmed false source facts, incomplete requirement/test contracts, and impossible or incomplete gates prevent approval. Ten claims FAIL; zero PASS; zero UNKNOWN.\n- P1 Source accuracy: FAIL. Findings F01, F07, F13.\n- P2 Requirements accuracy: FAIL. F02, F03, F04.\n- P4 Scope completeness: FAIL. F07.\n- P5 Decision completeness: FAIL. F05, F06, F07, F08, F12.\n## Findings, evidence, and exact fixes\n{\"overallVerdict\":\"DISAGREE\",\"accuracy\":84,\"completeness\":68,\"confidence\":93,\"passCount\":0,\"failCount\":10,\"unknownCount\":0,\"failList\":[\"P1\",\"P2\",\"P3\",\"P4\",\"P5\",\"P6\",\"P7\",\"P8\",\"P9\",\"P10\"],\"unknownList\":[],\"implementerFixes\":[\"F01\",\"F02\",\"F03\",\"F04\",\"F07\",\"F08\",\"F10\",\"F11\",\"F13\",\"F14\",\"F15\",\"F16\"],\"operatorDecisionFails\":[\"F05:D1\",\"F06:D2\",\"F09:D3\",\"F12:D4\"],\"headSha\":\"edbba968eecea07e8e96bd250c93065c07f3a2e3\",\"receiptPaths\":[\"docs/receipts/hv/hostile-validator-plan-readiness-20261009T145521Z.md\",\"docs/receipts/hv/hostile-validator-plan-readiness-20261009T145521Z.json\",\"docs/receipts/hv/20261009T145521Z-plan-readiness-r1.request.jsonl\",\"docs/receipts/hv/20261009T145521Z-plan-readiness-r1.response.jsonl\"],\"reviewerSessionId\":\"Codex-20261009T145732Z-plugin-session\",\"reviewerRequestId\":\"req-20261009T145734Z-plan-readiness-hv\"}\n=== hostile-validator-plan-readiness-r2-20261009T160546Z.md lines=71 ===\n- OverallVerdict: **DISAGREE**. The plan remains **NOT APPROVED**.\n- Accuracy: **90/100**; Completeness: **78/100**; Confidence: **96/100**.\n- **P1 Source accuracy: FAIL.** Plan line 70 states `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` in a stdio MCP subprocess “equals `session_id`.” [Claude's current environment-variable reference](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/env-vars) says a long-lived MCP subprocess retains the ID from spawn while hooks and shell subprocesses update it on `/clear`; on resume without an explicit ID it may also retain the startup ID. This is a false unconditional host-binding assertion. The current REST route and typed client also prove the mint contract cannot be implemented as written (finding R2-01). Other sampled source anchors and repository heads agreed with HEAD. **Fix:** make the stdio `/clear` and resume behavior explicit and provide a current-session key handoff; specify the actual mint route. **Classification:** implementer plan edit preserving D1/D2.\n- **P2 Requirements accuracy: FAIL.** New and existing IDs were checked for collisions and existence, but the plan's literal mapping instructions contain nonexistent shortened TR/TEST IDs (R2-04). **Fix:** use full canonical IDs in every mapping or specify a deterministic expansion before `createMapping`. **Classification:** implementer plan edit.\n- **P3 Locked decisions: FAIL.** D3 and D4 are applied. D1's exact-one request rule is contradicted by independent random nonces on duplicate hook delivery (R2-02); D2's same-host attribution rule is contradicted by the shared workspace fallback and stale stdio session key (R2-03/R2-05). **Fix:** preserve D1/D2 and make ingress idempotency and binding reliable across all eight hosts. **Classification:** implementer plan edit; the queued-message contingency in R2-08 may need an operator choice if the spike disproves arrival-time hooks.\n- **P4 Completeness of scope: FAIL.** Server, client, REPL, PowerShell and Node cores, all eight plugins, and SQLite/PostgreSQL/SQL Server migrations have named tasks. Codex hook trust is a required activation step absent from the rollout (R2-06), and fallback host binding is incomplete (R2-03). **Fix:** add explicit activation/trust and live tests; close each host's no-key attribution path. **Classification:** implementer plan edit.\n- **P5 Decision completeness: FAIL.** The mint route, duplicate delivery guarantee, cross-session fallback, and queued-message no-hook branch leave implementers to choose behavior (R2-01/02/03/08). **Fix:** lock the route, idempotency, fail-closed/reliable binding, and queued arrival contingency. **Classification:** mostly implementer edits; R2-08 is conditional operator decision.\n- **P6 Executability: FAIL.** Named build targets and test paths exist, but the REST begin route cannot receive an omitted path ID (R2-01), and `dotnet ef migrations add` is required without a toolchain preflight or installation instruction for `dotnet-ef` (R2-07). The host also has SDK 10.0.111 against `global.json` 10.0.201, with Pester 5 and bats absent, correctly flagged by P0.1. **Fix:** specify a viable mint route and pinned EF tooling in P0.1. **Classification:** implementer plan edit.\n- **P7 Testability: FAIL.** Many ACs have named tests, including score boundaries and BDPv4 mock negative checks, but the plan requires a RED receipt for existing behavior deliberately checked green in P0 (R2-09). It also lacks live verification that Codex hooks are trusted and that a missing host key never crosses sessions. **Fix:** distinguish baseline-green ACs from new RED obligations and add those negative/live tests. **Classification:** implementer plan edit.\n- **P8 Internal consistency: FAIL.** The 30 tasks and dependencies are enumerated, yet “every request exactly one turn” conflicts with duplicate random nonce delivery and warning-only detection, and the blanket RED rule conflicts with P0's existing-green ACs (R2-02/09). **Fix:** reconcile those assertions and acceptance gates. **Classification:** implementer plan edit.\n**R2-01 - Ingress REST route cannot omit `requestId` (P3/P5/P6).** Plan “Requirements catalog” line 155, TR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-004 line 300, “Wire contracts” line 486, A1 line 510, and “New surfaces” line 505 require the existing begin-turn REST route to mint with an omitted ID while also saying “No new REST routes.” HEAD `src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Controllers/SessionLogController.cs:388-396` declares `[HttpPost(\"{agent}/{sessionId}/{requestId}/begin\")]` and requires `string requestId`; `src/QBrainAi.Client/SessionLogClient.cs:174-191` requires and interpolates that ID. No body `ingressNonce` can supply a missing URL segment. `docs/context/session-log-schema.md` also says a first persist requires `planFile` and `todoId`, which the failsafe mint payload in line 486 omits. **Make PASS:** define a separate no-ID mint route (or a fully specified compatible routing contract), body including validated context/sentinels and nonce, exact return/duplicate behavior, and REST/client/MCP/REPL tests; update “No new REST routes” and rollout accordingly. **Implementer edit.**\n**R2-02 - Random nonce does not ensure one turn per delivered request (P3/P5/P8).** Plan “Locked operator decisions” lines 22, 27, 29, “Host binding table” lines 69, 71, 73, 75, and A7 line 522 say a no-ID host generates one random nonce per hook *process* and prevents duplicate delivery by registration, then merely warns after two mints. Separate executions for one host event get different nonces and create two turns; the warning fires after durable duplicates. The A3/A8 tests cover retries reusing one nonce, not two processes receiving one event. **Make PASS:** specify and test a durable, unique event identity or a demonstrated single-delivery boundary for each no-ID host, including two independent processes receiving one event; never count warning-only detection as D1 compliance. **Implementer edit preserving D1.**\n**R2-03 - Workspace pointer may attribute a write to another host session (P3/P4/P5/P7).** Plan D2 line 30, host table lines 69-77, TR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-003 line 299, wire contract line 487, and A4 line 516 bind unknown-key verbs to one workspace pointer with `host_session_unknown`; Copilot uses it by design and Cowork/Codex/Grok can use it. Two simultaneous sessions in one workspace can point at different requests, but one pointer holds only the latest. The warning does not preserve origin. **Make PASS:** give every supported verb a verified invocation-scoped host key or refuse to write when attribution cannot be established; test concurrent sessions with no key and assert zero wrong-turn writes. **Implementer edit preserving D2.**\n**R2-04 - Mapping shorthand is not a valid requirement ID (P2/P6).** Plan P0 step 4 line 134 says pass mappings “as listed” to `workflow.requirements.createMapping`. “Mappings” lines 350-370 use `TR-SESSIONTURN-001`, `TEST-SESSIONREVIEW-003`, and suffixes like `-002`, while created records are `TR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001`, `TEST-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-003`. Line 143 only defines shorthand for ACs, not mappings. `src/QBrainAi.Repl.Core/RequirementsWorkflow.cs:464-510` validates then fetches each exact referenced ID, so literal rows fail. **Make PASS:** write every full TR/TEST ID in every row, or prescribe a precise expansion and validate the expanded IDs before creating mappings. **Implementer edit.**\n**R2-05 - Claude stdio session key becomes stale on `/clear` (P1/P3/P4).** Plan host table line 70 claims the env key equals hook `session_id` for stdio MCP verbs. [Claude's env-var documentation](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/env-vars) explicitly says hooks and shell subprocesses update on `/clear`, while a stdio MCP subprocess retains its spawn ID; resume without explicit ID may also use the startup ID. A verb from the new session can therefore bind to the old session's active request. **Make PASS:** specify propagation of the current hook session ID into the long-lived MCP connection or another invocation key; test `/clear`, resume, and concurrent session cases. **Implementer edit preserving D2.**\n**R2-06 - Codex hook trust is absent from activation (P4/P6/P7).** Plan host table line 72, A9 line 526, and rollout line 555 add `hooks/hooks.json` and retire manual enforcement after sync. [Official Codex plugin documentation](https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/plugins) says enabling a plugin does not trust its bundled hooks; Codex skips them until the user reviews and trusts that hook definition. A synced but untrusted plugin logs no prompt turns. **Make PASS:** add an operator-facing trust activation/check to A9 and rollout, keep manual logging until proven active, and require a fresh Codex prompt's hook event and server turn as a G6/live assertion. **Implementer edit.**\n**R2-07 - EF migration tool absent from toolchain gate (P6).** Plan P0.1 line 131 preflights SDK, Pester, bats, node/npm, while A1 line 510 and S3 line 534 require `dotnet ef migrations add`. `.config/dotnet-tools.json` pins only `docfx`; `dotnet tool list --global` showed no `dotnet-ef`, and `dotnet ef --version` failed on this host. P0.1's `dotnet-install.sh` example also conflicts with the workspace/operator PowerShell-only execution rule. **Make PASS:** pin/preflight `dotnet-ef` and specify a PowerShell-run installation/restore path and exact provider migration command; use a PowerShell SDK installation path. **Implementer edit.**\n**R2-08 - Queued-message arrival branch unresolved (P5).** Plan D1 line 29 defines ingress as when a message *arrives*; P0.2 line 132 merely checks how queued messages reach UserPromptSubmit, and risk line 560 says it is unverified. No task says what to do if that hook runs only when a queued message starts processing, or lacks its own prompt ID. **Make PASS:** lock a branch that captures arrival before execution and name its implementation/tests, or explicitly present the required host limitation to the operator. **Conditional operator decision:** if the spike disproves arrival-time delivery, choose (a) build a host arrival bridge and retain D1, or (b) revise D1's arrival requirement. The current locked D1 favors (a).\n**R2-09 - P0 baseline-green ACs cannot have RED receipts (P7/P8).** Plan AC allocation lines 476-477 says P0 creates FR-SUPPORT-010G ac-1..ac-4 and TR-SUPPORT-010G ac-1 for *existing* behavior, verifies them with existing `SessionLogServiceReplaceDeleteTests`, then says **every AC above** requires a failing RED receipt before code. P0 explicitly makes no product change or new tests, and those existing tests already pass. **Make PASS:** exempt documented baseline-green ACs from RED and require existing green evidence, while preserving RED/mocks-green/negative-check order for new or changed ACs; otherwise define a real failing test for them. **Implementer edit.**\n- Implementer plan fixes: R2-01 through R2-07 and R2-09; provide evidence or accurately mark R2-10. These preserve the operator's D1-D4 rulings. In particular, do not replace D1 with prompt-text dedupe or D2 with warning-only cross-session attribution.\n- Operator decision if P0.2 disproves arrival-time queued hooks: R2-08, host arrival bridge versus an explicit revision of D1. The plan needs to state this branch before approval.\n=== hostile-validator-plan-readiness-r3-20261009T164531Z.md lines=60 ===\n- OverallVerdict: **DISAGREE**. The plan remains **NOT APPROVED**.\n- Accuracy: **90/100**. Most verified inventory and contract claims are correct, but one source claim is false and the attribution contract contradicts the requested behavior.\n- Completeness: **82/100**. Queue lifecycle, hook-poor ingress, and baseline test coverage remain open.\nThe revised source has **591 lines, 479 non-empty**. The live TODO description has **479 lines, 479 ordinal-equal** to those non-empty source lines; `done=false`, note says NOT APPROVED, and there are **30** implementationTasks. The handoff reports these counts and the status accurately. The revised source explicitly fixes R2-01 (new mint route and required context), R2-02 (event-derived nonces), R2-03/R2-05 (live-session refusal and stale stdio key), R2-04 (full canonical mapping ids), R2-06 (Codex hook trust), R2-07 (pinned local dotnet-ef and PowerShell install), R2-09 (baseline-green exception), and R2-10's *statement* of uncertainty and P0.1 retrieval task. R2-08/D5 now has a concrete Claude arrival bridge. A sampled real transcript `enqueue` record has keys `type, operation, timestamp, sessionId, content` and no `source_uuid`; deriving its nonce from those fields is faithful to the earliest observed enqueue record. These textual corrections do not prove the design works. Findings R3-01 through R3-07 below re-attack its behavior. The round-1 F01-F16 corrections and round-2 corrections were checked against their receipts and the revised sections; neither prior verdict authorizes approval.\n- **P1 Source accuracy: FAIL.** Line 151 says FR-SUPPORT-010G is referenced only in `SessionLogService.cs:1287-1317` and a retired handoff. `rg -n -F FR-SUPPORT-010G src tests` returns **36** references across **9** files, including `SessionLogController.cs:521`, `SessionLogClient.cs:222`, `FwhMcpTools.SessionLog.cs:177`, and two test projects. Other checked source anchors, HEAD, plugin local heads, and named migration directories agreed with the plan. See R3-05.\n- **P2 Requirements accuracy: PASS.** The plan declares 21 new FR, 23 TR, 23 TEST, and 21 full-ID mapping rows. A mechanical row check found no shortened/nonexistent proposed mapping id. Live `workflow.requirements.listFr/listTr/listTest` returned **352/480/512** records; the proposed ID families have no collision. The 33 existing-record check in the round-2 receipt remains applicable because src/docs HEAD has no product changes and the live counts are unchanged. P0 explicitly creates the new ids.\n- **P3 Locked decisions: FAIL.** D3's scoped use-case gate, D4's mandatory Other reason, and the D5 nonce derived from the earliest enqueue record are represented. But D2's request-that-caused-the-write rule is contradicted when D5 mint advances the active pointer before the queued request is processed (R3-01). The plan also exempts hook-poor hosts from D1 arrival timing without an operator ruling (R3-03).\n- **P4 Scope completeness: FAIL.** Server, client, REPL, MCP, both plugin cores, eight named plugins, and SQLite/PostgreSQL/SQL Server migrations have tasks and tests. However, the hostless/queued ingress exceptions and transcript removal lifecycle leave supported hosts without a complete implementation of the promised one-request-one-turn behavior (R3-02/03).\n- **P5 Decision completeness: FAIL.** The implementer must still decide when a queued mint becomes the write target, how to close a queued turn removed before delivery, and whether D1 may be weakened for hook-poor hosts. R3-01/02/03 give the missing decisions and permitted fixes.\n- **P7 Testability: FAIL.** A4 tests currently assert the wrong queued-message attribution (R3-01). A3 has no remove/popAll recovery test (R3-02). Baseline-green FR-SUPPORT-010G `ac-4` claims case-insensitive aliases and error text, but its only named test checks that an unknown section throws, not aliases or the message (R3-06). The Copilot duplicate-hook test feeds the same recorded payload twice, which does not establish that separately invoked host hooks receive an identical timestamp (R3-07).\n- **P8 Internal consistency: FAIL.** The task count and dependencies match. But the wire contract says `active-request.yaml` is written only by the prompt hook although D5 arrival bridge and hook-poor model mints must write it (R3-04). The classifier's blanket “hostile or subagent briefs” skip also contradicts the reviewer's required turn (R3-05).\n- **P9 Artifact parity: UNKNOWN.** TODO description and task count match the source, and handoff plan status/counts match. The handoff line 94 asserts group `triage-group-804b21086f3019ab` was filed in the worktree; read-only `workflow.triage.getGroup` returned not found with both the worktree and main workspace paths. The plan line 570 now correctly labels the group unverified and schedules P0.1 retrieval. The handoff has not adopted that uncertainty. See R3-08.\n- **P10 Risks: FAIL.** Nine ordinary triage report ids in lines 569-570 resolved through read-only `workflow.triage.getReport` with status `grouped`; the group remains unverified and scheduled. The arrival-bridge risk list omits the observed `remove` and `popAll` queue operations, which can leave a minted turn unprocessed and indefinitely in the stop ledger (R3-02). This risk has neither an in-plan lifecycle nor a triage id.\n**R3-01 - Queue mint steals work attribution (P3/P4/P5/P7/P8).** Plan “Locked operator decisions” line 31 makes the active request the most recently *minted* one; host table line 74 and FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC009 line 162 mint R2 at enqueue; FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-007-AC007 lines 205-207 and UC-1 line 422 explicitly send every later unqualified verb to R2. If the model is still executing R1, its next action was caused by R1, yet is written to R2. The A4 tests at line 524 assert this same wrong behavior. **Make PASS:** separate arrival-minted queued tokens from the host's currently processing request; advance write binding only on a verified processing transition or carry the invoking prompt identity, and keep the invocation binding immutable thereafter. Replace the A4/A8 tests with R1-work-after-R2-enqueue and R2-work-after-actual-delivery cases, including no explicit override. **Implementer plan edit preserving D1/D2/D5; no new operator decision.**\n**R3-02 - Removed queued messages have no lifecycle (P4/P5/P7/P10).** Plan lines 30, 74, 162, 303, 522, 568 only consume `queue-operation/enqueue`. Read-only parsing of the worktree Claude transcripts found **116 enqueue, 79 dequeue, 34 remove, 2 popAll** records; `remove` carries `content` and `reason`, while `dequeue` has no content. A removed entry can be minted but never claimed by UserPromptSubmit, and A7's ledger then blocks forever (lines 187-195). **Make PASS:** specify and test the turn state and attribution for `remove`/`popAll`, including how the model learns of and closes a never-processed minted request while retaining the one-turn and model-closes rules; define offset/mint/recovery ordering across bridge restart. Add fixture and live tests for remove/popAll. Add this observed format/lifecycle risk to line 568 and schedule its handling in A3/A7. **Implementer plan edit; if the author wants a host hook to close such turns instead, that is an operator decision because it changes the locked model-closes rule.**\n**R3-03 - D1 arrival is waived for supported hosts (P3/P4/P5).** Line 29 says every user message is minted when it arrives, but lines 75, 79-80, 136, and 165 set Cowork without hooks, classic Cline, and possibly cline-v2 queued messages to mint only when the model first sees them. A host limitation shown to the operator is not approval of an exception; D5 explicitly chose an arrival bridge to keep D1 literal. **Make PASS:** provide arrival integrations and tests for those supported modes, or list the exact exceptions as an explicit operator decision with options: (A) preserve D1 by building a host arrival adapter, or (B) approve a scoped first-sight waiver for named hosts. **Operator decision required for option B; implementation design for option A may be edited by the implementer.**\n**R3-04 - Wire contract contradicts its mint writers (P8).** Line 495 says `sessions/<hostSessionKey>/active-request.yaml` is written “only by the prompt hook mint”; lines 31 and 305 say the arrival bridge or model `mintTurn` also writes it. **Make PASS:** after R3-01 is resolved, name distinct queued-token and processing-target files and specify exactly which event writes each; update wire contracts, A3/A4/A8 tasks, and tests consistently. **Implementer plan edit.**\n**R3-05 - Genuine hostile review request is classified as a system event, and a source claim is false (P1/P8).** FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-002-AC001 line 170 and A3 line 522 skip “hostile or subagent briefs” by content, while line 123 requires the Codex hostile reviewer to write its own MCP turn. The current review brief is a user prompt to Codex and needs that turn. A structural subagent marker (`agent_id`, `MCP_SUBAGENT_ID`) is distinct from a textual brief. Separately, line 151's “referenced only” claim is contradicted by **36** src/test references in **9** files (`rg -n -F FR-SUPPORT-010G src tests`). **Make PASS:** remove content-based hostile/subagent-brief exclusion and test a user-supplied hostile brief opens a turn while a true marked subagent event does not; correct line 151 to list the actual service, controller, client, MCP, and test references. **Implementer plan edit.**\n**R3-06 - Baseline-green ac-4 is not verified (P7).** FR-SUPPORT-010G `ac-4` line 155 promises every alias, case insensitivity, and an error naming valid sections. Lines 352, 442, and 483 allocate only `SessionLogServiceReplaceDeleteTests.ReplaceTurnSectionAsync_UnknownSection_Throws`. That test at `tests/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Tests/Services/SessionLogServiceReplaceDeleteTests.cs:272-282` asserts only `ArgumentException`, while source `SessionLogService.cs:1300-1317` implements the broader aliases. **Make PASS:** add named characterization tests for every alias/case variant and the valid-section error text to the P0 baseline-green gate, or narrow the AC to the tested behavior and create a separately tested new AC. Update TEST-SUPPORT-010G and P0's no-new-tests wording accordingly. **Implementer plan edit.**\n**R3-07 - Copilot nonce identity is not established (P3/P7).** Host table line 77 hashes `sessionId`, `timestamp`, and `prompt`; line 522 feeds the *same recorded payload* to two test processes. The [Copilot hooks reference](https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/hooks-reference) defines `timestamp` as a hook input, but does not promise a stable per-message id across independently registered callbacks. The plan's guarantee at lines 29 and 161 depends on such stability. This is an inference risk, not proof that Copilot emits different values. **Make PASS:** capture two real host deliveries for one event and test that their nonce inputs match, or enforce a single trusted delivery boundary/another stable host message key; make a mismatched-timestamp duplicate a negative test. **Implementer plan edit/evidence.**\n**R3-08 - Filed triage group is unverified (P9 UNKNOWN).** Plan “Risks” line 570 correctly says `triage-group-804b21086f3019ab` status is unverified and P0.1 line 135 schedules getGroup. Handoff line 94 still asserts it was filed in the worktree. `workflow.triage.getGroup` with `groupId` and `workspacePath` set to the worktree, then the main workspace, returned `McpNotFoundException` both times. This does not prove it was never filed. **Make PASS:** obtain a retrievable read-only group receipt from its actual scope, or change the handoff to state “unverified” and retain the scheduled read-only check. **Implementer handoff edit/evidence; no triage mutation in this review.**\nPrimary host references: [Claude Code hook input and `prompt_id`](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks), [Copilot hook payload](https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/hooks-reference), [Codex bundled-hook trust](https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/plugins), [Codex hook lifecycle](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/hooks). These sources support host-field observations; R3-07 is explicitly an inference about the missing stability guarantee.\n{\"overallVerdict\":\"DISAGREE\",\"accuracy\":90,\"completeness\":82,\"confidence\":96,\"passCount\":1,\"failCount\":7,\"unknownCount\":2,\"failList\":[\"P1\",\"P3\",\"P4\",\"P5\",\"P7\",\"P8\",\"P10\"],\"unknownList\":[\"P6\",\"P9\"],\"implementerFixes\":[\"R3-01\",\"R3-02\",\"R3-04\",\"R3-05\",\"R3-06\",\"R3-07\",\"R3-08\"],\"operatorDecisionFails\":[\"R3-03: D1 waiver for named hook-poor hosts requires operator choice\",\"R3-02 only if host-side closure replaces model closure\"],\"headSha\":\"c47594498b6dd5e26f24617bb8cc72f7132af642\",\"receiptPaths\":[\"docs/receipts/hv/hostile-validator-plan-readiness-r3-20261009T164531Z.md\",\"docs/receipts/hv/hostile-validator-plan-readiness-r3-20261009T164531Z.json\",\"docs/receipts/hv/20261009T164531Z-plan-readiness-r3.request.jsonl\",\"docs/receipts/hv/20261009T164531Z-plan-readiness-r3.response.jsonl\"],\"reviewerSessionId\":\"Codex-20261009T032846Z-plugin-session\",\"reviewerRequestId\":\"req-20261009T164531Z-plan-readiness-r3\"}\n=== hostile-validator-plan-readiness-r4-20261009T174055Z.md lines=59 ===\nOverallVerdict: **DISAGREE**. Accuracy: **88/100**. Completeness: **76/100**. Confidence: **96/100**. This unapproved plan is not ready for operator approval or agent execution without design work. Claim counts: 2 PASS, 8 FAIL, 0 UNKNOWN. These scores assess plan readiness, not the implementer's conduct or the excluded infrastructure and Pi3 monitor.\n- **P1 Source accuracy: PASS.** The sampled and cited code surfaces, types, routes, hook shapes, build targets, source locations, plugin versions, transcript operation shapes, and FR-SUPPORT-010G citations checked against HEAD or named plugin sources. The 36 FR-SUPPORT-010G citations in nine files are present. Current live triage status is assessed under P9/P10, not as a source-tree claim. The plan still has logical defects described below.\n- **P2 Requirements accuracy: PASS.** Live lists returned 352 FR, 480 TR, and 512 TEST records; proposed SESSIONTURN/SESSIONCLASS/SESSIONREVIEW/SUPPORT-010G ids were absent from the live store and docs, are assigned to P0, and the sampled existing ids/mappings resolve. This is not an approval to create them.\n- **P3 Locked operator decisions: FAIL.** F-R4-03 makes D5 queue lifecycle non-idempotent; F-R4-04 breaks D2 causal attribution after a later message is delivered; F-R4-05 leaves D1/D6 Copilot arrival registration incompletely checked; F-R4-06 contradicts D6. D3/D4 and the 98/98 HV protocol are represented faithfully where checked.\n- **P4 Scope completeness: FAIL.** F-R4-01 permits Phase A/B plugin gates to pass while none of the eight repos is synchronized; F-R4-05 omits supported Copilot registration surfaces. The server/client/REPL/MCP/PowerShell/Node/provider work is otherwise listed, including three migration providers and host unsupported paths.\n- **P5 Decision completeness: FAIL.** F-R4-04 leaves the central causal binding problem unsolved after R2 delivery. F-R4-03 leaves crash-consistent identity for non-enqueue queue events unspecified. F-R4-05 does not define behavior for a policy-level duplicate registration that the installer cannot repair.\n- **P6 Executability: FAIL.** F-R4-01: the exact sync and validation commands have a successful zero-plugin path in this worktree. The SDK, Pester, bats and dotnet-ef are not currently installed at the specified versions; P0.1 explicitly provisions them, so their current absence is not a separate plan defect. The named Nuke targets and test projects otherwise exist.\n- **P7 Testability: FAIL.** F-R4-02 has no repeated-Stop regression and its AC itself specifies the bypass. F-R4-03 lacks identical-content crash replay tests for remove/popAll. F-R4-04 only tests before/after R2 delivery, not resumption of R1-caused work. F-R4-01's G6 cannot prove eight-repo coverage as written. F-R4-05 omits duplicate sources. HV-P0, HV-A, HV-B and 98/98 are stated consistently.\n- **P8 Internal consistency: FAIL.** F-R4-06's model-invoked mint test directly contradicts D6 and the ingress contract. F-R4-02's stop-active pass contradicts the stop gate's stated block-until-closed behavior. F-R4-01 contradicts the asserted all-eight-plugin sync and G6 gates. Phase and 30-task ordering otherwise match.\n- **P9 Artifact parity: FAIL.** Plan/TODO text and 30 tasks match, and status/counts/locations in the handoff match. F-R4-07: the handoff's current group status and the plan's triage note are stale relative to the now-readable live group. This matters because the handoff is the next agent's entry point.\n- **P10 Risks: FAIL.** F-R4-03's duplicate queue-event replay risk is absent from the risk register despite the text claiming replay is idempotent. F-R4-02's repeated-Stop bypass is also absent. F-R4-07 changes the claimed triage verification status; read-only lookup now proves the group and one report exist, while other listed ids could not all be independently resolved during this review.\n**F-R4-01 - Plugin sync can pass with zero target repositories.** Plan: plugin matrix/parent notes lines 92-97; A9 line 560, S8-S9 lines 578-580, S11 line 584, rollout line 589. Exact defect: `./build.ps1 SyncAgentPlugins` and `ValidatePluginPowerShellOnly` omit `--agent-plugin-parent` while asserting all eight sibling repos are synchronized and validated. In this `.claude/worktrees/session-20261009` location, `build/Build.SyncAgentPlugins.cs:170-196` resolves the plugin parent as `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer`, whereas the eight repos live under `/home/sharpninja/github`. Lines 49-54 warn and successfully return when discovery is empty; lines 75-86 validate the staged package alone. The plan's version and G6 assertions therefore can be false green. **Make PASS:** pass `--agent-plugin-parent /home/sharpninja/github` in every named sync/validation invocation; require discovery and version/core-hash assertions for exactly the eight named repos; add a Build.Tests regression for `.claude/worktrees` resolution or use the explicit override throughout. **Classification:** implementer plan edit; no operator decision.\n**F-R4-02 - Second Stop invocation bypasses open-turn enforcement.** Plan: FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC006 line 218, wire contract line 518, A7 line 556. Exact defect: `stop_hook_active` returns `{}` while keeping the ledger, even if an earlier Stop block found an open turn. Claude Code documents that `stop_hook_active=true` means a prior Stop hook blocked continuation; returning `{}` then allows stop. This defeats AC001/AC009 and the live acceptance text (line 602). Official evidence: [Claude Code hooks reference](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks) sections on Stop input and `decision: \"block\"`; it also documents the continuation limit. **Make PASS:** evaluate the unresolved ledger on every Stop invocation and return a block while turns remain open, with explicit behavior and receipt if the host's continuation cap is reached; add a two-Stop test with a still-open turn, then a closure/pass test. **Classification:** implementer plan edit; allowing one retry to end with open turns would require an operator decision to weaken enforcement.\n**F-R4-03 - Replaying a queue removal can mutate the next identical request.** Plan: bridge algorithm line 76, AC010 near line 187, A3 line 548, queue wire line 521, risk line 594. Exact defect: the bridge applies a record and only then advances `arrival-bridge.offset`. Its `remove`/`popAll` target is the oldest queued entry with matching content hash; only re-read `enqueue` is explicitly idempotent. After a crash between a `remove absorbed_mid_turn` or withdrawal and offset advance, replay can match a second intentionally identical queued message and deliver/withdraw it. A3's generic \"re-applies it idempotently\" test misses this counterexample. Two separate identical prompts must remain two separate turns under D1/D5. **Make PASS:** assign each queue-operation record a stable event identity/offset and durably record that it was applied atomically with the queued/write-target mutation, or otherwise guarantee idempotent replay for every operation. Add two-identical-enqueue crash fixtures for `remove absorbed_mid_turn`, other/no-reason `remove`, and `popAll`, and update the risk section. **Classification:** implementer plan edit; the mechanism is a design choice for the implementer but must be specified in the plan before approval.\n**F-R4-04 - Latest-delivered binding cannot always identify the causing request.** Plan: D2 lines 27 and 32, A4 line 550, use cases around line 448. Exact defect: after R2 is delivered/absorbed, the plan makes R2 the write target for every later verb, even if the model continues R1-caused work. The proposed explicit R1 override requires the model to name R1, while the operator chose automatic attribution through host hooks. The tests cover R1 work before R2 delivery and immediate R2 work after delivery, but not R1 work resumed after R2. The statement that every action maps to the request that caused it is therefore unproved and can be false. **Make PASS:** specify an automatic per-invocation causal identity that distinguishes resumed R1 work, including behavior when a host cannot supply it, and add an interleaving test R1→R2→R1 without an explicit override. If the operator wants to redefine D2 as latest-delivered/manual override, list that exact relaxation as an operator decision with those options rather than silently choosing it. **Classification:** implementer plan edit to honor D2; operator decision only if requesting the relaxation.\n**F-R4-05 - Copilot duplicate registration inventory is incomplete.** Plan: Copilot binding line 79, A9 line 560. Exact defect: line 79 calls repo `hooks/hooks.json`, the plugin manifest, and the user hook directory \"every Copilot hook source.\" The [GitHub Copilot hooks reference](https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/hooks-reference) also names policy, `.github/hooks/*.json`, repository and user settings including `.claude/settings.json`, and installed plugins. Separate commands can receive distinct timestamps, so the timestamp-derived nonce cannot deduplicate all duplicates. A policy hook may be non-removable. **Make PASS:** enumerate and test every supported source, detect duplicates regardless of source, repair those owned by the plugin, and define fail-closed `turn_logging_unsupported` or operator remediation for a non-removable duplicate; include differing-timestamp fixtures for each source. **Classification:** implementer plan edit; if policy conflicts require accepting duplicate turns, that is an operator decision with fail-closed versus weakened D1 options.\n**F-R4-06 - A4 retains a prohibited model mint path.** Plan: locked D1/D6 lines 29 and 31, A4 line 550. Exact defect: D6 forbids first-sight/model minting for supported hosts, but A4 still requires `model mintTurn mid-turn registers without cancel`. This can drive an implementation that opens a turn only when the model notices a message. **Make PASS:** replace the test with a host arrival/prompt-hook mint followed by independent edits, or explicitly label a model mint as a diagnostic invocation that cannot satisfy host ingress and cannot register a production turn. **Classification:** implementer plan edit; no new operator decision.\n**F-R4-07 - Triage group status in the plan and handoff is stale.** Plan: risks line 596; handoff `docs/handoffs/sessionturn-review-plan-20261009T022141Z.md:94`. Exact defect: the plan says `workflow.triage.getGroup` returns not found and status remains unverified; the handoff presents the same as the next agent's current state. Read-only `workflow.triage.getGroup` now returns `triage-group-804b21086f3019ab` in the worktree, `status: failed`, `reportCount: 1`, and grouped report `triage-report-611ca1b413ea44ff946672f79bd7e850`; processing failed with agent exit 1. This proves filing, not successful processing or relocation. Other listed triage ids were not all resolvable on this run, so their present store status remains unverified. **Make PASS:** update plan and handoff with the observed group/report/status and retain relocation/processing as pending; do not rewrite history as successful triage. **Classification:** implementer documentation edit; no operator decision or triage mutation in this review.\n- R3-01: minting alone now preserves R1's write target until R2 delivery, and A4 tests before delivery. Residual F-R4-04 remains after delivery.\n- R3-02: queued/delivered/withdrawn and remove/popAll shapes are represented, with withdrawn stop block. Residual F-R4-03 remains on crash replay.\n- R3-03/D6: host adapters and unsupported behavior are stated for Cowork/classic Cline/cline-v2. F-R4-06 is the contradictory A4 test; Copilot inventory F-R4-05 is still incomplete.\n- R3-04: `write-target.yaml` and `queued.yaml` replace the active pointer as claimed. PASS for that correction.\n- R3-05: structural system markers and the hostile-review-user-prompt counterexample appear in A3; 36 FR-SUPPORT-010G citations in nine files were found. PASS for that correction.\n- R3-06: baseline-green ac-4 is narrowed to its named assertion. PASS for that correction.\n- R3-07: Copilot has a proposed single boundary and differing-timestamp negative test, but F-R4-05 shows the source inventory is incomplete.\n- R3-08: the handoff accurately recorded the group's unverified status at the prior review; read-only lookup during this review now establishes F-R4-07's newer status.\n- Nonce-source failure: plan requires `turn-open-failed` with `nonce_source_missing` and no invented nonce; this correction is represented. The C1-C12 QB coordination section and MEMORY-PROCESS-007 protocol are mostly order-independent; C4 inherits F-R4-04's flawed binding. QB-AGENT-001 itself was not scored.\n=== hostile-validator-plan-readiness-r5-20261009T181414Z.md lines=99 ===\n- **P1 FAIL:** Current-state plugin inventory contains false known-list, hash, checkout, and branch-count assertions (F-R5-03).\n- **P2 FAIL:** The new SESSIONTURN/SESSIONCLASS/SESSIONREVIEW families are collision-free in the 352 FR/480 TR/512 TEST live store, and 21 FR/23 TR/23 TEST with 21 mappings are 