{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_106","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command '\"'$p=\"/home/sharpninja/.claude/plans/create-a-new-plan-giggly-music.md\"; $a=Get-Content -LiteralPath $p; foreach($n in @(29,30,31,63,74,80,82,83,85,87,88,89,119,168,169,174,191,200,215,251,277,280,305,308,340,367,379,382,389,394,406,410,439,539,569,573,595,597,617,621,623,629)){\"$n`t$($a[$n-1])\"}'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_106","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command '\"'$p=\"/home/sharpninja/.claude/plans/create-a-new-plan-giggly-music.md\"; $a=Get-Content -LiteralPath $p; foreach($n in @(29,30,31,63,74,80,82,83,85,87,88,89,119,168,169,174,191,200,215,251,277,280,305,308,340,367,379,382,389,394,406,410,439,539,569,573,595,597,617,621,623,629)){\"$n`t$($a[$n-1])\"}'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"29\t- Request identity (operator D1, 2026-10-09: \"have the server issue a request token when a message arrives\"): the server mints every turn's `requestId` when a user message arrives (ingress), through the new mint operation `POST /qbrainai/sessionlog/{agent}/{sessionId}/mint`. The mint call carries an idempotency nonce derived from the host's own identity for that message event (host binding table): a host per-prompt id, or a SHA-256 of host event fields that every delivery of that event carries identically. Every delivery of one event therefore sends the same nonce and receives the same token, including two hook processes started by a doubly registered hook, network retries, and failsafe replay; two separate user messages (including two intentionally identical prompts) are two events and receive two tokens. The host id is only the idempotency key; the turn identity is the server-issued token. There is no prompt-text dedupe window. Every mint comes from a host arrival mechanism (a prompt hook, the arrival bridge, or a host-native hook adapter); no supported host relies on the model to mint. The stop gate's `duplicate_delivery_suspected` warning is a diagnostic only and is never counted as D1 compliance.\n30\t- Queued-message arrival (operator D5, 2026-10-09: \"Arrival bridge\"): a message the operator sends while a turn is running is minted when it is enqueued, not when the model later processes it. On hosts that record the enqueue in a transcript (Claude Code), a per-host-session arrival bridge started by the SessionStart hook tails the transcript and follows each queued message through the lifecycle the transcript records. Observed in this workspace's Claude Code transcripts on 2026-10-09: 117 `enqueue`, 79 `dequeue`, 35 `remove` (27 with reason `absorbed_mid_turn`), 2 `popAll`; a normal prompt is `enqueue` then `dequeue` within milliseconds, a mid-turn message is `enqueue` then `remove` with `absorbed_mid_turn` when it is injected into the running turn (for example the operator's mid-turn message beginning \"write a global memory with the\" was removed with `absorbed_mid_turn` at 2026-10-09T14:35:47.730Z), and `popAll` pulls queued text back into the input box. The bridge mints at `enqueue` (state `queued`); the request becomes `delivered` at `dequeue` (the prompt hook then claims the mint) or at `remove` with `absorbed_mid_turn`; it becomes `withdrawn` at `popAll` or a `remove` with any other or no reason. Task notifications and other system events that the host also enqueues are classified and never minted.\n31\t- Hosts without an arrival mechanism (operator D6, 2026-10-09: \"Adapters or unsupported\"): every host gets an arrival adapter wherever the host offers any mechanism (prompt hook, transcript enqueue record, host-native hook, message-queue event). A host with no arrival mechanism is declared unsupported for turn logging until it gains one: its plugin reports `turn_logging_unsupported` on every session-log verb and writes nothing, and no first-sight minting is used anywhere.\n63\tNine repos: the eight listed in `build/Build.SyncAgentPlugins.cs:19-28` plus `mcpserver-grok-bot-plugin` (D8; A9 adds it to that list; see also `docs/AGENT-PLUGIN-AVAILABILITY.md` and `docs/AGENT-PLUGIN-FEATURE-MATRIX.md`). State re-verified 2026-10-09T18:48Z by a read-only inventory (`git rev-parse`, `git status --porcelain`, `sha256sum lib/plugin-hook.ps1`, `CORE-MANIFEST.yaml`, `git ls-remote --symref`): all nine are checked out under `/home/sharpninja/github`; remote default branches are `main` for eight and `master` for opencode; six are on their default branch at 1.118.0 with `lib/plugin-hook.ps1` SHA-256 prefix `e4cd6116`, except grok-bot, whose file hashes to `d917feb6` although its `CORE-MANIFEST.yaml:14` declares `e4cd6116` (core drift that A9's sync must resolve and its receipt must show fixed); codex, grok, and cline are detached at 1.108.0 with the older core `26137529`. The shared core today invents `req-<ts>-prompt-<rand4>` ids (`plugin-hook.ps1:865`), keeps one turn pointer per workspace and agent key (`resolve-cache-dir.ps1:64-68`, `repl-invoke.ps1:122,817`), reads the undocumented env `CLAUDE_STOP_HOOK_ACTIVE` (`plugin-hook.ps1:944`) instead of the payload `stop_hook_active`, and looks for `CLAUDE_SESSION_ID` (`agent-runtime-header.ps1:165-169`) although Claude Code sets `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`.\n74\t  - `mcpserver-opencode-plugin`: local on `master` at `0c9abc2` (remote default `master`), clean, 1.118.0, hook `e4cd6116`. Exports `createMcpServerPlugin` (also default; `src/index.ts:1`, `src/plugin.ts:148,258`), not `createQBrainAiPlugin`. Hooks `event`, `chat.message`, `tool.execute.before/after` (`plugin.ts:196-255`); its start regex (`plugin.ts:136-142`) matches no documented OpenCode event, so turn logging never starts, and `session.idle` (the real turn end) is unhandled; the factory treats OpenCode's plugin input as its own config (`plugin.ts:148`). jest via `npm test` (`node --experimental-vm-modules`, `package.json:16`).\n80\t- Queued-message arrival on every host (D5, D6): P0.2 records, per host, whether a message sent while a turn is running reaches an arrival mechanism at enqueue. Where the prompt event itself fires at enqueue, it is the arrival mechanism; where it fires only at processing time, the host's transcript or queue event is used through an arrival adapter with the same `queued`/`delivered`/`withdrawn` lifecycle as the Claude Code bridge; where neither exists, the host is unsupported for turn logging (`turn_logging_unsupported`).\n82\t- claude-code: nonce payload `prompt_id`; hook key payload `session_id`; verb key env `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` read at verb start in shell, PowerShell, and hook subprocesses (equals `session_id`; those subprocesses see the updated value after `/clear`); stdio MCP verbs use the live-session rule; payloads carrying `agent_id` are subagent events (system events, no turn); the subagent's own Bash and PowerShell verbs carry the parent's `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` and bind to the parent's write target; stop uses payload `stop_hook_active`. Arrival bridge (D5): started by SessionStart with the payload `transcript_path`; it processes `queue-operation` lines in file order. `enqueue` whose `content` the system-event classifier accepts as a user request: mint with nonce `q-` + SHA-256(`session_id` LF enqueue `timestamp` LF `content`) truncated to 62 hex characters, add `{ requestId, nonce, enqueueOffset, enqueueTimestamp, promptSha256, state: queued }` to the queue in `sessions/<key>/turn-state.yaml`. `dequeue` (no content): no state change by itself; the prompt hook that fires for that prompt, under `turn-open.lock`, claims the oldest `queued` entry whose `promptSha256` equals its prompt's hash, sets it `delivered`, and makes it the write target (it mints with `prompt_id` only when no entry matches). `remove` with reason `absorbed_mid_turn`: the oldest `queued` entry with that `content` hash becomes `delivered` and the write target. `remove` with any other or no reason, and `popAll` (each content line it carries): the matching `queued` entry becomes `withdrawn`. Any other operation or an unrecognized shape: warning `arrival_bridge_format_unrecognized`, no state change. Each record's state change and the advance of `appliedThroughOffset` past it are one atomic replace of `turn-state.yaml` (temp file, flush, rename, under `turn-open.lock`), and records that start below `appliedThroughOffset` are skipped, so a restart never re-applies `enqueue`, `remove`, or `popAll`; the server mint for `enqueue` happens before that replace and is idempotent (same nonce, `merged`). A `remove` or `popAll` targets the oldest `queued` entry whose `promptSha256` matches and whose `enqueueOffset` is below the record's offset.\n83\t- claude-cowork: same fields and arrival bridge as claude-code. P0.2 verifies that Cowork executes plugin hooks; if it does not, Cowork has no arrival mechanism and is unsupported for turn logging under D6 (`turn_logging_unsupported`, nothing written) until it gains one. A9 repairs the dangling `bin/mcpserver-stdio.cmd` connector.\n85\t- copilot: A9 points `plugin.json` at `hooks/hooks.json` and removes the dangling `.sh` hook file; nonce `cp-` + SHA-256(`sessionId` LF `timestamp` LF `prompt`) truncated to 61 hex characters, from the UserPromptSubmit payload fields the Copilot CLI hooks reference documents (`sessionId`, `timestamp` in Unix milliseconds, `cwd`, `prompt`; VS Code form `session_id`, ISO `timestamp`); hook key `sessionId`/`session_id`; verbs use the live-session rule (no session env var is documented). The reference does not promise that two separately registered hook commands receive an identical `timestamp`, so the Copilot adoption also enforces a single delivery boundary: an A2-equivalent check enumerates every source the Copilot hooks reference documents (policy `/etc/github-copilot/policy.d/*.json`; repository `.github/hooks/*.json`; user `~/.copilot/hooks/` honoring `COPILOT_HOME`; inline hooks in `.github/copilot/settings.json`, `.github/copilot/settings.local.json`, and the cross-tool `.claude/settings.json` and `.claude/settings.local.json`; `~/.copilot/settings.json`; plugin `hooks.json` or `hooks/hooks.json`; all sources run, and the Copilot cloud agent reads only `.github/hooks/*.json`), detects duplicate MCP prompt hooks across all of them, repairs the duplicates it owns (plugin, repository, and user files, with a backup), and for a duplicate it cannot remove (a policy file) fails closed: Copilot turn logging reports `turn_logging_unsupported` with operator remediation text, recorded as a D7 documented limitation; P0.2 captures two real deliveries of one prompt from two registered hooks when the Copilot CLI is available and records whether their `timestamp` values match (evidence only; D11 does not depend on it); A9 tests duplicate detection with one fixture per source, that with exactly one MCP prompt hook a delivery mints one turn, and that with two MCP prompt hooks registered (in any pair of sources, including two deliveries that differ only in `timestamp`) the prompt hook reports `turn_logging_unsupported` before any mint and the server receives zero mints (D11).\n87\t- grok-bot: same payload handling as grok (camelCase `promptId` nonce, `sessionId` hook key, `stopHookActive`, `subagentType`) until P0.2 records the Grok Bot cloud runtime's actual hook payload, session environment for shell commands, and queued-message behavior; each P0.2 outcome selects among the rules above (prompt-hook arrival, transcript or queue adapter, or a documented capability limitation per D7); verbs use `GROK_SESSION_ID` only if P0.2 proves it is set in the cloud runtime, else the live-session rule.\n88\t- cline (classic): the plugin itself is a stdio MCP server with no hooks, but the Cline app has native hooks (UserPromptSubmit with `prompt`; common fields `taskId`, `hookName`, `timestamp`, `workspaceRoots`, `userId`, `model`; seen in the local Cline source `/home/sharpninja/github/cline` at `b090c4a08`, `apps/vscode/src/core/hooks/hook-factory.ts:102-120,201-228` and `apps/vscode/proto/cline/hooks.proto:44-62`, not in public docs). Arrival adapter (D6): the cline plugin repo ships a Cline UserPromptSubmit hook script that mints through the Node core with nonce `cl-` + SHA-256(`taskId` LF `timestamp` LF `prompt`) truncated to 61 hex characters and host session key `taskId`; the MCP server's tools carry no `taskId`, so they use the live-session rule; single registration is enforced as for copilot. P0.2 verifies in the installed Cline build that native hooks run, where hook scripts are installed, the payload fields, and whether queued messages fire the hook at arrival; if no arrival mechanism exists, classic Cline is unsupported for turn logging under D6 (`turn_logging_unsupported`).\n89\t- cline-v2: turn opens in `beforeRun` with nonce `runId`; host session key `conversationId` (lifecycle snapshot and tool context; the plugin stops stripping `sessionId`, `conversationId`, `runId`, `toolCallId` at `plugin.ts:122-140`); prompt text from the last user entry of `snapshot.messages`; stop-equivalent `afterRun` (no per-run session close); `@cline/core` pin raised to the version shipping `@cline/shared` 0.0.92 types. P0.2 records whether a queued message starts its own run at arrival; if it does not, the arrival adapter uses the host's message-queue event if one exists; if neither exists, cline-v2 is unsupported for turn logging under D6 (`turn_logging_unsupported`).\n119\t- C3 Outcome mapping into any Node persistence path, including an audit outbox (TR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-008 AC-006): `created` and `merged` -> `persisted`; `skipped_duplicate` -> `unchanged`; `skipped_terminal` -> `rejected` with reason `skipped_terminal`, never retried or quarantined, failsafe record cleared; a server without per-turn outcomes -> `outcomeVerified:false`.\n168\t1. Toolchain gate on the execution host, recorded as a PlanningDecision: `dotnet --list-sdks` must include an SDK accepted by `global.json` (`10.0.201`, `rollForward: latestFeature`, so any 10.0.2xx or later feature band; on 2026-10-09 LAB-OMARCHY had only `10.0.111`, which fails `./build.ps1` with \"A compatible .NET SDK was not found\"), Pester 5 must be importable by `pwsh` (none installed on 2026-10-09), `bats` must be on PATH (missing on 2026-10-09), `node`/`npm` must satisfy each TypeScript repo's `engines` (node v26.8.2, npm 11.19.1 present), and `dotnet ef`